eads = _openmp_effective_n_threads()\n214 |     threads_per_worker = max(openmp_threads // xdist_worker_count, 1)\n215 |     threadpool_limits(threads_per_worker, user_api=\"openmp\")\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/conftest.py:pytest_configure`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n218 | def pytest_configure(config):\n219 |     # Use matplotlib agg backend during the tests including doctests\n220 |     try:\n221 |         import matplotlib\n222 | \n223 |         matplotlib.use(\"agg\")\n224 |     except ImportError:\n225 |         pass\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/utils/extmath.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/utils/extmath.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"visualization\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/model_selection/plot_cv_indices.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | Visualizing cross-validation behavior in scikit-learn\n  3 | =====================================================\n  4 | \n  5 | Choosing the right cross-validation object is a crucial part of fitting a\n  6 | model properly. There are many ways to split data into training and test\n  7 | sets in order to avoid model overfitting, to standardize the number of\n  8 | groups in test sets, etc.\n  9 | \n 10 | This example visualizes the behavior of several common scikit-learn objects\n 11 | for comparison.\n 12 | \"\"\"\n 13 | \n 14 | from sklearn.model_selection import (TimeSeriesSplit, KFold, ShuffleSplit,\n 15 |                                      StratifiedKFold, GroupShuffleSplit,\n 16 |                                      GroupKFold, StratifiedShuffleSplit,\n 17 |                                      StratifiedGroupKFold)\n 18 | import numpy as np\n 19 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 20 | from matplotlib.patches import Patch\n 21 | np.random.seed(1338)\n 22 | cmap_data = plt.cm.Paired\n 23 | cmap_cv = plt.cm.coolwarm\n 24 | n_splits = 4\n 25 | \n 26 | # %%\n 27 | # Visualize our data\n 28 | # ------------------\n 29 | #\n 30 | # First, we must understand the structure of our data. It has 100 randomly\n 31 | # generated input datapoints, 3 classes split unevenly across datapoints,\n 32 | # and 10 \"groups\" split evenly across datapoints.\n 33 | #\n 34 | # As we'll see, some cross-validation objects do specific things with\n 35 | # labeled data, others behave differently with grouped data, and others\n 36 | # do not use this information.\n 37 | #\n 38 | # To begin, we'll visualize our data.\n 39 | \n 40 | # Generate the class/group data\n 41 | n_points = 100\n 42 | X = np.random.randn(100, 10)\n 43 | \n 44 | percentiles_classes = [.1, .3, .6]\n 45 | y = np.hstack([[ii] * int(100 * perc)\n 46 |                for ii, perc in enumerate(percentiles_classes)])\n 47 | \n 48 | # Evenly spaced groups repeated once\n 49 | groups = np.hstack([[ii] * 10 for ii in range(10)])\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | =======================================\n  3 | Visualizing the stock market structure\n  4 | =======================================\n  5 | \n  6 | This example employs several unsupervised learning techniques to extract\n  7 | the stock market structure from variations in historical quotes.\n  8 | \n  9 | The quantity that we use is the daily variation in quote price: quotes\n 10 | that are linked tend to cofluctuate during a day.\n 11 | \n 12 | .. _stock_market:\n 13 | \n 14 | Learning a graph structure\n 15 | --------------------------\n 16 | \n 17 | We use sparse inverse covariance estimation to find which quotes are\n 18 | correlated conditionally on the others. Specifically, sparse inverse\n 19 | covariance gives us a graph, that is a list of connection. For each\n 20 | symbol, the symbols that it is connected too are those useful to explain\n 21 | its fluctuations.\n 22 | \n 23 | Clustering\n 24 | ----------\n 25 | \n 26 | We use clustering to group together quotes that behave similarly. Here,\n 27 | amongst the :ref:`various clustering techniques <clustering>` available\n 28 | in the scikit-learn, we use :ref:`affinity_propagation` as it does\n 29 | not enforce equal-size clusters, and it can choose automatically the\n 30 | number of clusters from the data.\n 31 | \n 32 | Note that this gives us a different indication than the graph, as the\n 33 | graph reflects conditional relations between variables, while the\n 34 | clustering reflects marginal properties: variables clustered together can\n 35 | be considered as having a similar impact at the level of the full stock\n 36 | market.\n 37 | \n 38 | Embedding in 2D space\n 39 | ---------------------\n 40 | \n 41 | For visualization purposes, we need to lay out the different symbols on a\n 42 | 2D canvas. For this we use :ref:`manifold` techniques to retrieve 2D\n 43 | embedding.\n 44 | \n 45 | \n 46 | Visualization\n 47 | -------------\n 48 | \n 49 | The output of the 3 models are combined in a 2D graph where nodes\n 50 | represents the stocks and edges the:\n 51 | \n 52 | - cluster labels are used to define the color of the nodes\n 53 | - the sparse covariance model is used to display the strength of the edges\n 54 | - the 2D embedding is used to position the nodes in the plan\n 55 | \n 56 | This example has a fair amount of visualization-related code, as\n 57 | visualization is crucial here to display the graph. One of the challenge\n 58 | is to position the labels minimizing overlap. For this we use an\n 59 | heuristic based on the direction of the nearest neighbor along each\n 60 | axis.\n 61 | \"\"\"\n 62 | \n 63 | # Author: Gael Varoquaux gael.varoquaux@normalesup.org\n 64 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 65 | \n 66 | import sys\n 67 | \n 68 | import numpy as np\n 69 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 70 | from matplotlib.collections import LineCollection\n 71 | \n 72 | import pandas as pd\n 73 | \n 74 | from sklearn import cluster, covariance, manifold\n 75 | \n 76 | print(__doc__)\n 77 | \n 78 | \n 79 | # #############################################################################\n 80 | # Retrieve the data from Internet\n 81 | \n 82 | # The data is from 2003 - 2008. This is reasonably calm: (not too long ago so\n 83 | # that we get high-tech firms, and before the 2008 crash). This kind of\n 84 | # historical data can be obtained for from APIs like the quandl.com and\n 85 | # alphavantage.co ones.\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `examples/miscellaneous/plot_roc_curve_visualization_api.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ================================\n 3 | ROC Curve with Visualization API\n 4 | ================================\n 5 | Scikit-learn defines a simple API for creating visualizations for machine\n 6 | learning. The key features of this API is to allow for quick plotting and\n 7 | visual adjustments without recalculation. In this example, we will demonstrate\n 8 | how to use the visualization API by comparing ROC curves.\n 9 | \"\"\"\n10 | print(__doc__)\n11 | \n12 | # %%\n13 | # Load Data and Train a SVC\n14 | # -------------------------\n15 | # First, we load the wine dataset and convert it to a binary classification\n16 | # problem. Then, we train a support vector classifier on a training dataset.\n17 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n18 | from sklearn.svm import SVC\n19 | from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n20 | from sklearn.metrics import plot_roc_curve\n21 | from sklearn.datasets import load_wine\n22 | from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n23 | \n24 | X, y = load_wine(return_X_y=True)\n25 | y = y == 2\n26 | \n27 | X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, random_state=42)\n28 | svc = SVC(random_state=42)\n29 | svc.fit(X_train, y_train)\n30 | \n31 | # %%\n32 | # Plotting the ROC Curve\n33 | # ----------------------\n34 | # Next, we plot the ROC curve with a single call to\n35 | # :func:`sklearn.metrics.plot_roc_curve`. The returned `svc_disp` object allows\n36 | # us to continue using the already computed ROC curve for the SVC in future\n37 | # plots.\n38 | svc_disp = plot_roc_curve(svc, X_test, y_test)\n39 | plt.show()\n40 | \n41 | # %%\n42 | # Training a Random Forest and Plotting the ROC Curve\n43 | # --------------------------------------------------------\n44 | # We train a random forest classifier and create a plot comparing it to the SVC\n45 | # ROC curve. Notice how `svc_disp` uses\n46 | # :func:`~sklearn.metrics.RocCurveDisplay.plot` to plot the SVC ROC curve\n47 | # without recomputing the values of the roc curve itself. Furthermore, we\n48 | # pass `alpha=0.8` to the plot functions to adjust the alpha values of the\n49 | # curves.\n50 | rfc = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=10, random_state=42)\n51 | rfc.fit(X_train, y_train)\n52 | ax = plt.gca()\n53 | rfc_disp = plot_roc_curve(rfc, X_test, y_test, ax=ax, alpha=0.8)\n54 | svc_disp.plot(ax=ax, alpha=0.8)\n55 | plt.show()\n56 | \n```\n\nFound function `examples/model_selection/plot_cv_indices.py:plot_cv_indices`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 75 | def plot_cv_indices(cv, X, y, group, ax, n_splits, lw=10):\n 76 |     \"\"\"Create a sample plot for indices of a cross-validation object.\"\"\"\n 77 | \n 78 |     # Generate the training/testing visualizations for each CV split\n 79 |     for ii, (tr, tt) in enumerate(cv.split(X=X, y=y, groups=group)):\n 80 |         # Fill in indices with the training/test groups\n 81 |         indices = np.array([np.nan] * len(X))\n 82 |         indices[tt] = 1\n 83 |         indices[tr] = 0\n 84 | \n 85 |         # Visualize the results\n 86 |         ax.scatter(range(len(indices)), [ii + .5] * len(indices),\n 87 |                    c=indices, marker='_', lw=lw, cmap=cmap_cv,\n 88 |                    vmin=-.2, vmax=1.2)\n 89 | \n 90 |     # Plot the data classes and groups at the end\n 91 |     ax.scatter(range(len(X)), [ii + 1.5] * len(X),\n 92 |                c=y, marker='_', lw=lw, cmap=cmap_data)\n 93 | \n 94 |     ax.scatter(range(len(X)), [ii + 2.5] * len(X),\n 95 |                c=group, marker='_', lw=lw, cmap=cmap_data)\n 96 | \n 97 |     # Formatting\n 98 |     yticklabels = list(range(n_splits)) + ['class', 'group']\n 99 |     ax.set(yticks=np.arange(n_splits+2) + .5, yticklabels=yticklabels,\n100 |            xlabel='Sample index', ylabel=\"CV iteration\",\n101 |            ylim=[n_splits+2.2, -.2], xlim=[0, 100])\n102 |     ax.set_title('{}'.format(type(cv).__name__), fontsize=15)\n103 |     return ax\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"visualization\", \"matplotlib visualization\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `examples/miscellaneous/plot_roc_curve_visualization_api.py`.\nFound file `examples/miscellaneous/plot_partial_dependence_visualization_api.py`.\nFound file `examples/miscellaneous/plot_display_object_visualization.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"matplotlib visualization\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from .base import _get_response\n  2 | \n  3 | from .. import average_precision_score\n  4 | from .. import precision_recall_curve\n  5 | \n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | \n  8 | \n  9 | class PrecisionRecallDisplay:\n 10 |     \"\"\"Precision Recall visualization.\n 11 | \n 12 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.plot_precision_recall_curve`\n 13 |     to create a visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 14 | \n 15 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 16 | \n 17 |     Parameters\n 18 |     -----------\n 19 |     precision : ndarray\n 20 |         Precision values.\n 21 | \n 22 |     recall : ndarray\n 23 |         Recall values.\n 24 | \n 25 |     average_precision : float, default=None\n 26 |         Average precision. If None, the average precision is not shown.\n 27 | \n 28 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 29 |         Name of estimator. If None, then the estimator name is not shown.\n 30 | \n 31 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 32 |         The class considered as the positive class. If None, the class will not\n 33 |         be shown in the legend.\n 34 | \n 35 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 36 | \n 37 |     Attributes\n 38 |     ----------\n 39 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 40 |         Precision recall curve.\n 41 | \n 42 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 43 |         Axes with precision recall curve.\n 44 | \n 45 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 46 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 47 | \n 48 |     See Also\n 49 |     --------\n 50 |     precision_recall_curve : Compute precision-recall pairs for different\n 51 |         probability thresholds.\n 52 |     plot_precision_recall_curve : Plot Precision Recall Curve for binary\n 53 |         classifiers.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Examples\n 56 |     --------\n 57 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 58 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import (precision_recall_curve,\n 60 |     ...                              PrecisionRecallDisplay)\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y,\n 65 |     ...                                                     random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0)\n 67 |     >>> clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 68 |     SVC(random_state=0)\n 69 |     >>> predictions = clf.predict(X_test)\n 70 |     >>> precision, recall, _ = precision_recall_curve(y_test, predictions)\n 71 |     >>> disp = PrecisionRecallDisplay(precision=precision, recall=recall)\n 72 |     >>> disp.plot()\n 73 |     <...>\n 74 |     >>> plt.show()\n 75 |     \"\"\"\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound code snippet in file `examples/model_selection/plot_cv_indices.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | Visualizing cross-validation behavior in scikit-learn\n  3 | =====================================================\n  4 | \n  5 | Choosing the right cross-validation object is a crucial part of fitting a\n  6 | model properly. There are many ways to split data into training and test\n  7 | sets in order to avoid model overfitting, to standardize the number of\n  8 | groups in test sets, etc.\n  9 | \n 10 | This example visualizes the behavior of several common scikit-learn objects\n 11 | for comparison.\n 12 | \"\"\"\n 13 | \n 14 | from sklearn.model_selection import (TimeSeriesSplit, KFold, ShuffleSplit,\n 15 |                                      StratifiedKFold, GroupShuffleSplit,\n 16 |                                      GroupKFold, StratifiedShuffleSplit,\n 17 |                                      StratifiedGroupKFold)\n 18 | import numpy as np\n 19 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 20 | from matplotlib.patches import Patch\n 21 | np.random.seed(1338)\n 22 | cmap_data = plt.cm.Paired\n 23 | cmap_cv = plt.cm.coolwarm\n 24 | n_splits = 4\n 25 | \n 26 | # %%\n 27 | # Visualize our data\n 28 | # ------------------\n 29 | #\n 30 | # First, we must understand the structure of our data. It has 100 randomly\n 31 | # generated input datapoints, 3 classes split unevenly across datapoints,\n 32 | # and 10 \"groups\" split evenly across datapoints.\n 33 | #\n 34 | # As we'll see, some cross-validation objects do specific things with\n 35 | # labeled data, others behave differently with grouped data, and others\n 36 | # do not use this information.\n 37 | #\n 38 | # To begin, we'll visualize our data.\n 39 | \n 40 | # Generate the class/group data\n 41 | n_points = 100\n 42 | X = np.random.randn(100, 10)\n 43 | \n 44 | percentiles_classes = [.1, .3, .6]\n 45 | y = np.hstack([[ii] * int(100 * perc)\n 46 |                for ii, perc in enumerate(percentiles_classes)])\n 47 | \n 48 | # Evenly spaced groups repeated once\n 49 | groups = np.hstack([[ii] * 10 for ii in range(10)])\n```\n\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from .base import _get_response\n  2 | \n  3 | from .. import auc\n  4 | from .. import roc_curve\n  5 | \n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | \n  8 | \n  9 | class RocCurveDisplay:\n 10 |     \"\"\"ROC Curve visualization.\n 11 | \n 12 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.plot_roc_curve` to create a\n 13 |     visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 14 | \n 15 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 16 | \n 17 |     Parameters\n 18 |     ----------\n 19 |     fpr : ndarray\n 20 |         False positive rate.\n 21 | \n 22 |     tpr : ndarray\n 23 |         True positive rate.\n 24 | \n 25 |     roc_auc : float, default=None\n 26 |         Area under ROC curve. If None, the roc_auc score is not shown.\n 27 | \n 28 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 29 |         Name of estimator. If None, the estimator name is not shown.\n 30 | \n 31 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 32 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing the roc auc\n 33 |         metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is considered\n 34 |         as the positive class.\n 35 | \n 36 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 37 | \n 38 |     Attributes\n 39 |     ----------\n 40 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 41 |         ROC Curve.\n 42 | \n 43 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 44 |         Axes with ROC Curve.\n 45 | \n 46 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 47 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 48 | \n 49 |     See Also\n 50 |     --------\n 51 |     roc_curve : Compute Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n 52 |     plot_roc_curve : Plot Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n 53 |     roc_auc_score : Compute the area under the ROC curve.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Examples\n 56 |     --------\n 57 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 58 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn import metrics\n 60 |     >>> y = np.array([0, 0, 1, 1])\n 61 |     >>> pred = np.array([0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8])\n 62 |     >>> fpr, tpr, thresholds = metrics.roc_curve(y, pred)\n 63 |     >>> roc_auc = metrics.auc(fpr, tpr)\n 64 |     >>> display = metrics.RocCurveDisplay(fpr=fpr, tpr=tpr, roc_auc=roc_auc,\\\n 65 |                                           estimator_name='example estimator')\n 66 |     >>> display.plot()\n 67 |     <...>\n 68 |     >>> plt.show()\n 69 |     \"\"\"\n 70 | \n 71 |     def __init__(self, *, fpr, tpr, roc_auc=None, estimator_name=None, pos_label=None):\n 72 |         self.estimator_name = estimator_name\n 73 |         self.fpr = fpr\n 74 |         self.tpr = tpr\n 75 |         self.roc_auc = roc_auc\n 76 |         self.pos_label = pos_label\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | import scipy as sp\n  2 | \n  3 | from .base import _get_response\n  4 | \n  5 | from .. import det_curve\n  6 | from .._base import _check_pos_label_consistency\n  7 | \n  8 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  9 | from ...utils import deprecated\n 10 | \n 11 | \n 12 | class DetCurveDisplay:\n 13 |     \"\"\"DET curve visualization.\n 14 | \n 15 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.DetCurveDisplay.from_estimator`\n 16 |     or :func:`~sklearn.metrics.DetCurveDisplay.from_predictions` to create a\n 17 |     visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 18 | \n 19 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 20 | \n 21 |     .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 22 | \n 23 |     Parameters\n 24 |     ----------\n 25 |     fpr : ndarray\n 26 |         False positive rate.\n 27 | \n 28 |     fnr : ndarray\n 29 |         False negative rate.\n 30 | \n 31 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 32 |         Name of estimator. If None, the estimator name is not shown.\n 33 | \n 34 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 35 |         The label of the positive class.\n 36 | \n 37 |     Attributes\n 38 |     ----------\n 39 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 40 |         DET Curve.\n 41 | \n 42 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 43 |         Axes with DET Curve.\n 44 | \n 45 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 46 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 47 | \n 48 |     See Also\n 49 |     --------\n 50 |     det_curve : Compute error rates for different probability thresholds.\n 51 |     DetCurveDisplay.from_estimator : Plot DET curve given an estimator and\n 52 |         some data.\n 53 |     DetCurveDisplay.from_predictions : Plot DET curve given the true and\n 54 |         predicted labels.\n 55 | \n 56 |     Examples\n 57 |     --------\n 58 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 60 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import det_curve, DetCurveDisplay\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=1000, random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n 65 |     ...     X, y, test_size=0.4, random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)\n 67 |     >>> y_pred = clf.decision_function(X_test)\n 68 |     >>> fpr, fnr, _ = det_curve(y_test, y_pred)\n 69 |     >>> display = DetCurveDisplay(\n 70 |     ...     fpr=fpr, fnr=fnr, estimator_name=\"SVC\"\n 71 |     ... )\n 72 |     >>> display.plot()\n 73 |     <...>\n 74 |     >>> plt.show()\n 75 |     \"\"\"\n 76 | \n 77 |     def __init__(self, *, fpr, fnr, estimator_name=None, pos_label=None):\n 78 |         self.fpr = fpr\n 79 |         self.fnr = fnr\n 80 |         self.estimator_name = estimator_name\n 81 |         self.pos_label = pos_label\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound code snippet in file `examples/applications/svm_gui.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==========\n  3 | Libsvm GUI\n  4 | ==========\n  5 | \n  6 | A simple graphical frontend for Libsvm mainly intended for didactic\n  7 | purposes. You can create data points by point and click and visualize\n  8 | the decision region induced by different kernels and parameter settings.\n  9 | \n 10 | To create positive examples click the left mouse button; to create\n 11 | negative examples click the right button.\n 12 | \n 13 | If all examples are from the same class, it uses a one-class SVM.\n 14 | \n 15 | \"\"\"\n 16 | \n 17 | print(__doc__)\n 18 | \n 19 | # Author: Peter Prettenhoer <peter.prettenhofer@gmail.com>\n 20 | #\n 21 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 22 | \n 23 | import matplotlib\n 24 | matplotlib.use('TkAgg')\n 25 | from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import FigureCanvasTkAgg\n 26 | try:\n 27 |     from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import NavigationToolbar2Tk\n 28 | except ImportError:\n 29 |     # NavigationToolbar2TkAgg was deprecated in matplotlib 2.2\n 30 |     from matplotlib.backends.backend_tkagg import (\n 31 |         NavigationToolbar2TkAgg as NavigationToolbar2Tk\n 32 |     )\n 33 | from matplotlib.figure import Figure\n 34 | from matplotlib.contour import ContourSet\n 35 | \n 36 | import sys\n 37 | import numpy as np\n 38 | import tkinter as Tk\n 39 | \n 40 | from sklearn import svm\n 41 | from sklearn.datasets import dump_svmlight_file\n 42 | \n 43 | y_min, y_max = -50, 50\n 44 | x_min, x_max = -50, 50\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the calibration module specifically:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/calibration.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/calibration.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/calibration.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/calibration.py`.\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"Calibration of predicted probabilities.\"\"\"\n  2 | \n  3 | # Author: Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n  4 | #         Balazs Kegl <balazs.kegl@gmail.com>\n  5 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n  6 | #         Mathieu Blondel <mathieu@mblondel.org>\n  7 | #\n  8 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n  9 | \n 10 | import warnings\n 11 | from inspect import signature\n 12 | from functools import partial\n 13 | \n 14 | from math import log\n 15 | import numpy as np\n 16 | from joblib import Parallel\n 17 | \n 18 | from scipy.special import expit\n 19 | from scipy.special import xlogy\n 20 | from scipy.optimize import fmin_bfgs\n 21 | \n 22 | from .base import (\n 23 |     BaseEstimator,\n 24 |     ClassifierMixin,\n 25 |     RegressorMixin,\n 26 |     clone,\n 27 |     MetaEstimatorMixin,\n 28 | )\n 29 | from .preprocessing import label_binarize, LabelEncoder\n 30 | from .utils import (\n 31 |     column_or_1d,\n 32 |     deprecated,\n 33 |     indexable,\n 34 | )\n 35 | \n 36 | from .utils.multiclass import check_classification_targets\n 37 | from .utils.fixes import delayed\n 38 | from .utils.validation import check_is_fitted, check_consistent_length\n 39 | from .utils.validation import _check_sample_weight, _num_samples\n 40 | from .utils import _safe_indexing\n 41 | from .isotonic import IsotonicRegression\n 42 | from .svm import LinearSVC\n 43 | from .model_selection import check_cv, cross_val_predict\n 44 | \n 45 | \n 46 | class CalibratedClassifierCV(ClassifierMixin, MetaEstimatorMixin, BaseEstimator):\n 47 |     \"\"\"Probability calibration with isotonic regression or logistic regression.\n 48 | \n 49 |     This class uses cross-validation to both estimate the parameters of a\n 50 |     classifier and subsequently calibrate a classifier. With default\n 51 |     `ensemble=True`, for each cv split it\n 52 |     fits a copy of the base estimator to the training subset, and calibrates it\n 53 |     using the testing subset. For prediction, predicted probabilities are\n 54 |     averaged across these individual calibrated classifiers. When\n 55 |     `ensemble=False`, cross-validation is used to obtain unbiased predictions,\n 56 |     via :func:`~sklearn.model_selection.cross_val_predict`, which are then\n 57 |     used for calibration. For prediction, the base estimator, trained using all\n 58 |     the data, is used. This is the method implemented when `probabilities=True`\n 59 |     for :mod:`sklearn.svm` estimators.\n 60 | \n 61 |     Already fitted classifiers can be calibrated via the parameter\n 62 |     `cv=\"prefit\"`. In this case, no cross-validation is used and all provided\n 63 |     data is used for calibration. The user has to take care manually that data\n 64 |     for model fitting and calibration are disjoint.\n 65 | \n 66 |     The calibration is based on the :term:`decision_function` method of the\n 67 |     `base_estimator` if it exists, else on :term:`predict_proba`.\n 68 | \n 69 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Parameters\n 72 |     ----------\n 73 |     base_estimator : estimator instance, default=None\n 74 |         The classifier whose output need to be calibrated to provide more\n 75 |         accurate `predict_proba` outputs. The default classifier is\n 76 |         a :class:`~sklearn.svm.LinearSVC`.\n 77 | \n 78 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}, default='sigmoid'\n 79 |         The method to use for calibration. Can be 'sigmoid' which\n 80 |         corresponds to Platt's method (i.e. a logistic regression model) or\n 81 |         'isotonic' which is a non-parametric approach. It is not advised to\n 82 |         use isotonic calibration with too few calibration samples\n 83 |         ``(<<1000)`` since it tends to overfit.\n 84 | \n 85 |     cv : int, cross-validation generator, iterable or \"prefit\", \\\n 86 |             default=None\n 87 |         Determines the cross-validation splitting strategy.\n 88 |         Possible inputs for cv are:\n 89 | \n 90 |         - None, to use the default 5-fold cross-validation,\n 91 |         - integer, to specify the number of folds.\n 92 |         - :term:`CV splitter`,\n 93 |         - An iterable yielding (train, test) splits as arrays of indices.\n 94 | \n 95 |         For integer/None inputs, if ``y`` is binary or multiclass,\n 96 |         :class:`~sklearn.model_selection.StratifiedKFold` is used. If ``y`` is\n 97 |         neither binary nor multiclass, :class:`~sklearn.model_selection.KFold`\n 98 |         is used.\n 99 | \n100 |         Refer to the :ref:`User Guide <cross_validation>` for the various\n101 |         cross-validation strategies that can be used here.\n102 | \n103 |         If \"prefit\" is passed, it is assumed that `base_estimator` has been\n104 |         fitted already and all data is used for calibration.\n105 | \n106 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.22\n107 |             ``cv`` default value if None changed from 3-fold to 5-fold.\n108 | \n109 |     n_jobs : int, default=None\n110 |         Number of jobs to run in parallel.\n111 |         ``None`` means 1 unless in a :obj:`joblib.parallel_backend` context.\n112 |         ``-1`` means using all processors.\n113 | \n114 |         Base estimator clones are fitted in parallel across cross-validation\n115 |         iterations. Therefore parallelism happens only when `cv != \"prefit\"`.\n116 | \n117 |         See :term:`Glossary <n_jobs>` for more details.\n118 | \n119 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n120 | \n121 |     ensemble : bool, default=True\n122 |         Determines how the calibrator is fitted when `cv` is not `'prefit'`.\n123 |         Ignored if `cv='prefit'`.\n124 | \n125 |         If `True`, the `base_estimator` is fitted using training data and\n126 |         calibrated using testing data, for each `cv` fold. The final estimator\n127 |         is an ensemble of `n_cv` fitted classifer and calibrator pairs, where\n128 |         `n_cv` is the number of cross-validation folds. The output is the\n129 |         average predicted probabilities of all pairs.\n130 | \n131 |         If `False`, `cv` is used to compute unbiased predictions, via\n132 |         :func:`~sklearn.model_selection.cross_val_predict`, which are then\n133 |         used for calibration. At prediction time, the classifier used is the\n134 |         `base_estimator` trained on all the data.\n135 |         Note that this method is also internally implemented  in\n136 |         :mod:`sklearn.svm` estimators with the `probabilities=True` parameter.\n137 | \n138 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n139 | \n140 |     Attributes\n141 |     ----------\n142 |     classes_ : ndarray of shape (n_classes,)\n143 |         The class labels.\n144 | \n145 |     n_features_in_ : int\n146 |         Number of features seen during :term:`fit`. Only defined if the\n147 |         underlying base_estimator exposes such an attribute when fit.\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n150 | \n151 |     feature_names_in_ : ndarray of shape (`n_features_in_`,)\n152 |         Names of features seen during :term:`fit`. Only defined if the\n153 |         underlying base_estimator exposes such an attribute when fit.\n154 | \n155 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n156 | \n157 |     calibrated_classifiers_ : list (len() equal to cv or 1 if `cv=\"prefit\"` \\\n158 |             or `ensemble=False`)\n159 |         The list of classifier and calibrator pairs.\n160 | \n161 |         - When `cv=\"prefit\"`, the fitted `base_estimator` and fitted\n162 |           calibrator.\n163 |         - When `cv` is not \"prefit\" and `ensemble=True`, `n_cv` fitted\n164 |           `base_estimator` and calibrator pairs. `n_cv` is the number of\n165 |           cross-validation folds.\n166 |         - When `cv` is not \"prefit\" and `ensemble=False`, the `base_estimator`,\n167 |           fitted on all the data, and fitted calibrator.\n168 | \n169 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.24\n170 |             Single calibrated classifier case when `ensemble=False`.\n171 | \n172 |     Examples\n173 |     --------\n174 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n175 |     >>> from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\n176 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\n177 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=100, n_features=2,\n178 |     ...                            n_redundant=0, random_state=42)\n179 |     >>> base_clf = GaussianNB()\n180 |     >>> calibrated_clf = CalibratedClassifierCV(base_estimator=base_clf, cv=3)\n181 |     >>> calibrated_clf.fit(X, y)\n182 |     CalibratedClassifierCV(base_estimator=GaussianNB(), cv=3)\n183 |     >>> len(calibrated_clf.calibrated_classifiers_)\n184 |     3\n185 |     >>> calibrated_clf.predict_proba(X)[:5, :]\n186 |     array([[0.110..., 0.889...],\n187 |            [0.072..., 0.927...],\n188 |            [0.928..., 0.071...],\n189 |            [0.928..., 0.071...],\n190 |            [0.071..., 0.928...]])\n191 | \n192 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n193 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=100, n_features=2,\n194 |     ...                            n_redundant=0, random_state=42)\n195 |     >>> X_train, X_calib, y_train, y_calib = train_test_split(\n196 |     ...        X, y, random_state=42\n197 |     ... )\n198 |     >>> base_clf = GaussianNB()\n199 |     >>> base_clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n200 |     GaussianNB()\n201 |     >>> calibrated_clf = CalibratedClassifierCV(\n202 |     ...     base_estimator=base_clf,\n203 |     ...     cv=\"prefit\"\n204 |     ... )\n205 |     >>> calibrated_clf.fit(X_calib, y_calib)\n206 |     CalibratedClassifierCV(base_estimator=GaussianNB(), cv='prefit')\n207 |     >>> len(calibrated_clf.calibrated_classifiers_)\n208 |     1\n209 |     >>> calibrated_clf.predict_proba([[-0.5, 0.5]])\n210 |     array([[0.936..., 0.063...]])\n211 | \n212 |     References\n213 |     ----------\n214 |     .. [1] Obtaining calibrated probability estimates from decision trees\n215 |            and naive Bayesian classifiers, B. Zadrozny & C. Elkan, ICML 2001\n216 | \n217 |     .. [2] Transforming Classifier Scores into Accurate Multiclass\n218 |            Probability Estimates, B. Zadrozny & C. Elkan, (KDD 2002)\n219 | \n220 |     .. [3] Probabilistic Outputs for Support Vector Machines and Comparisons to\n221 |            Regularized Likelihood Methods, J. Platt, (1999)\n222 | \n223 |     .. [4] Predicting Good Probabilities with Supervised Learning,\n224 |            A. Niculescu-Mizil & R. Caruana, ICML 2005\n225 |     \"\"\"\n226 | \n227 |     def __init__(\n228 |         self,\n229 |         base_estimator=None,\n230 |         *,\n231 |         method=\"sigmoid\",\n232 |         cv=None,\n233 |         n_jobs=None,\n234 |         ensemble=True,\n235 |     ):\n236 |         self.base_estimator = base_estimator\n237 |         self.method = method\n238 |         self.cv = cv\n239 |         self.n_jobs = n_jobs\n240 |         self.ensemble = ensemble\n241 | \n242 |     def fit(self, X, y, sample_weight=None):\n243 |         \"\"\"Fit the calibrated model.\n244 | \n245 |         Parameters\n246 |         ----------\n247 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n248 |             Training data.\n249 | \n250 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n251 |             Target values.\n252 | \n253 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n254 |             Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n255 | \n256 |         Returns\n257 |         -------\n258 |         self : object\n259 |             Returns an instance of self.\n260 |         \"\"\"\n261 |         check_classification_targets(y)\n262 |         X, y = indexable(X, y)\n263 | \n264 |         if self.base_estimator is None:\n265 |             # we want all classifiers that don't expose a random_state\n266 |             # to be deterministic (and we don't want to expose this one).\n267 |             base_estimator = LinearSVC(random_state=0)\n268 |         else:\n269 |             base_estimator = self.base_estimator\n270 | \n271 |         self.calibrated_classifiers_ = []\n272 |         if self.cv == \"prefit\":\n273 |             # `classes_` should be consistent with that of base_estimator\n274 |             check_is_fitted(self.base_estimator, attributes=[\"classes_\"])\n275 |             self.classes_ = self.base_estimator.classes_\n276 | \n277 |             pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(base_estimator)\n278 |             n_classes = len(self.classes_)\n279 |             predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes)\n280 | \n281 |             calibrated_classifier = _fit_calibrator(\n282 |                 base_estimator,\n283 |                 predictions,\n284 |                 y,\n285 |                 self.classes_,\n286 |                 self.method,\n287 |                 sample_weight,\n288 |             )\n289 |             self.calibrated_classifiers_.append(calibrated_classifier)\n290 |         else:\n291 |             # Set `classes_` using all `y`\n292 |             label_encoder_ = LabelEncoder().fit(y)\n293 |             self.classes_ = label_encoder_.classes_\n294 |             n_classes = len(self.classes_)\n295 | \n296 |             # sample_weight checks\n297 |             fit_parameters = signature(base_estimator.fit).parameters\n298 |             supports_sw = \"sample_weight\" in fit_parameters\n299 |             if sample_weight is not None:\n300 |                 sample_weight = _check_sample_weight(sample_weight, X)\n301 |                 if not supports_sw:\n302 |                     estimator_name = type(base_estimator).__name__\n303 |                     warnings.warn(\n304 |                         f\"Since {estimator_name} does not support \"\n305 |                         \"sample_weights, sample weights will only be\"\n306 |                         \" used for the calibration itself.\"\n307 |                     )\n308 | \n309 |             # Check that each cross-validation fold can have at least one\n310 |             # example per class\n311 |             if isinstance(self.cv, int):\n312 |                 n_folds = self.cv\n313 |             elif hasattr(self.cv, \"n_splits\"):\n314 |                 n_folds = self.cv.n_splits\n315 |             else:\n316 |                 n_folds = None\n317 |             if n_folds and np.any(\n318 |                 [np.sum(y == class_) < n_folds for class_ in self.classes_]\n319 |             ):\n320 |                 raise ValueError(\n321 |                     f\"Requesting {n_folds}-fold \"\n322 |                     \"cross-validation but provided less than \"\n323 |                     f\"{n_folds} examples for at least one class.\"\n324 |                 )\n325 |             cv = check_cv(self.cv, y, classifier=True)\n326 | \n327 |             if self.ensemble:\n328 |                 parallel = Parallel(n_jobs=self.n_jobs)\n329 | \n330 |                 self.calibrated_classifiers_ = parallel(\n331 |                     delayed(_fit_classifier_calibrator_pair)(\n332 |                         clone(base_estimator),\n333 |                         X,\n334 |                         y,\n335 |                         train=train,\n336 |                         test=test,\n337 |                         method=self.method,\n338 |                         classes=self.classes_,\n339 |                         supports_sw=supports_sw,\n340 |                         sample_weight=sample_weight,\n341 |                     )\n342 |                     for train, test in cv.split(X, y)\n343 |                 )\n344 |             else:\n345 |                 this_estimator = clone(base_estimator)\n346 |                 _, method_name = _get_prediction_method(this_estimator)\n347 |                 pred_method = partial(\n348 |                     cross_val_predict,\n349 |                     estimator=this_estimator,\n350 |                     X=X,\n351 |                     y=y,\n352 |                     cv=cv,\n353 |                     method=method_name,\n354 |                     n_jobs=self.n_jobs,\n355 |                 )\n356 |                 predictions = _compute_predictions(\n357 |                     pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes\n358 |                 )\n359 | \n360 |                 if sample_weight is not None and supports_sw:\n361 |                     this_estimator.fit(X, y, sample_weight)\n362 |                 else:\n363 |                     this_estimator.fit(X, y)\n364 |                 calibrated_classifier = _fit_calibrator(\n365 |                     this_estimator,\n366 |                     predictions,\n367 |                     y,\n368 |                     self.classes_,\n369 |                     self.method,\n370 |                     sample_weight,\n371 |                 )\n372 |                 self.calibrated_classifiers_.append(calibrated_classifier)\n373 | \n374 |         first_clf = self.calibrated_classifiers_[0].base_estimator\n375 |         if hasattr(first_clf, \"n_features_in_\"):\n376 |             self.n_features_in_ = first_clf.n_features_in_\n377 |         if hasattr(first_clf, \"feature_names_in_\"):\n378 |             self.feature_names_in_ = first_clf.feature_names_in_\n379 |         return self\n380 | \n381 |     def predict_proba(self, X):\n382 |         \"\"\"Calibrated probabilities of classification.\n383 | \n384 |         This function returns calibrated probabilities of classification\n385 |         according to each class on an array of test vectors X.\n386 | \n387 |         Parameters\n388 |         ----------\n389 |         X : The samples, as accepted by base_estimator.predict_proba\n390 | \n391 |         Returns\n392 |         -------\n393 |         C : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_classes)\n394 |             The predicted probas.\n395 |         \"\"\"\n396 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n397 |         # Compute the arithmetic mean of the predictions of the calibrated\n398 |         # classifiers\n399 |         mean_proba = np.zeros((_num_samples(X), len(self.classes_)))\n400 |         for calibrated_classifier in self.calibrated_classifiers_:\n401 |             proba = calibrated_classifier.predict_proba(X)\n402 |             mean_proba += proba\n403 | \n404 |         mean_proba /= len(self.calibrated_classifiers_)\n405 | \n406 |         return mean_proba\n407 | \n408 |     def predict(self, X):\n409 |         \"\"\"Predict the target of new samples. The predicted class is the\n410 |         class that has the highest probability, and can thus be different\n411 |         from the prediction of the uncalibrated classifier.\n412 | \n413 |         Parameters\n414 |         ----------\n415 |         X : The samples, as accepted by base_estimator.predict\n416 | \n417 |         Returns\n418 |         -------\n419 |         C : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n420 |             The predicted class.\n421 |         \"\"\"\n422 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n423 |         return self.classes_[np.argmax(self.predict_proba(X), axis=1)]\n424 | \n425 |     def _more_tags(self):\n426 |         return {\n427 |             \"_xfail_checks\": {\n428 |                 \"check_sample_weights_invariance\": (\n429 |                     \"zero sample_weight is not equivalent to removing samples\"\n430 |                 ),\n431 |             }\n432 |         }\n433 | \n434 | \n435 | def _fit_classifier_calibrator_pair(\n436 |     estimator, X, y, train, test, supports_sw, method, classes, sample_weight=None\n437 | ):\n438 |     \"\"\"Fit a classifier/calibration pair on a given train/test split.\n439 | \n440 |     Fit the classifier on the train set, compute its predictions on the test\n441 |     set and use the predictions as input to fit the calibrator along with the\n442 |     test labels.\n443 | \n444 |     Parameters\n445 |     ----------\n446 |     estimator : estimator instance\n447 |         Cloned base estimator.\n448 | \n449 |     X : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_features)\n450 |         Sample data.\n451 | \n452 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n453 |         Targets.\n454 | \n455 |     train : ndarray, shape (n_train_indicies,)\n456 |         Indices of the training subset.\n457 | \n458 |     test : ndarray, shape (n_test_indicies,)\n459 |         Indices of the testing subset.\n460 | \n461 |     supports_sw : bool\n462 |         Whether or not the `estimator` supports sample weights.\n463 | \n464 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n465 |         Method to use for calibration.\n466 | \n467 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n468 |         The target classes.\n469 | \n470 |     sample_weight : array-like, default=None\n471 |         Sample weights for `X`.\n472 | \n473 |     Returns\n474 |     -------\n475 |     calibrated_classifier : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n476 |     \"\"\"\n477 |     X_train, y_train = _safe_indexing(X, train), _safe_indexing(y, train)\n478 |     X_test, y_test = _safe_indexing(X, test), _safe_indexing(y, test)\n479 |     if supports_sw and sample_weight is not None:\n480 |         sw_train = _safe_indexing(sample_weight, train)\n481 |         sw_test = _safe_indexing(sample_weight, test)\n482 |     else:\n483 |         sw_train = None\n484 |         sw_test = None\n485 | \n486 |     if supports_sw:\n487 |         estimator.fit(X_train, y_train, sample_weight=sw_train)\n488 |     else:\n489 |         estimator.fit(X_train, y_train)\n490 | \n491 |     n_classes = len(classes)\n492 |     pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(estimator)\n493 |     predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X_test, n_classes)\n494 | \n495 |     calibrated_classifier = _fit_calibrator(\n496 |         estimator, predictions, y_test, classes, method, sample_weight=sw_test\n497 |     )\n498 |     return calibrated_classifier\n499 | \n500 | \n501 | def _get_prediction_method(clf):\n502 |     \"\"\"Return prediction method.\n503 | \n504 |     `decision_function` method of `clf` returned, if it\n505 |     exists, otherwise `predict_proba` method returned.\n506 | \n507 |     Parameters\n508 |     ----------\n509 |     clf : Estimator instance\n510 |         Fitted classifier to obtain the prediction method from.\n511 | \n512 |     Returns\n513 |     -------\n514 |     prediction_method : callable\n515 |         The prediction method.\n516 |     method_name : str\n517 |         The name of the prediction method.\n518 |     \"\"\"\n519 |     if hasattr(clf, \"decision_function\"):\n520 |         method = getattr(clf, \"decision_function\")\n521 |         return method, \"decision_function\"\n522 |     elif hasattr(clf, \"predict_proba\"):\n523 |         method = getattr(clf, \"predict_proba\")\n524 |         return method, \"predict_proba\"\n525 |     else:\n526 |         raise RuntimeError(\n527 |             \"'base_estimator' has no 'decision_function' or 'predict_proba' method.\"\n528 |         )\n529 | \n530 | \n531 | def _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes):\n532 |     \"\"\"Return predictions for `X` and reshape binary outputs to shape\n533 |     (n_samples, 1).\n534 | \n535 |     Parameters\n536 |     ----------\n537 |     pred_method : callable\n538 |         Prediction method.\n539 | \n540 |     method_name: str\n541 |         Name of the prediction method\n542 | \n543 |     X : array-like or None\n544 |         Data used to obtain predictions.\n545 | \n546 |     n_classes : int\n547 |         Number of classes present.\n548 | \n549 |     Returns\n550 |     -------\n551 |     predictions : array-like, shape (X.shape[0], len(clf.classes_))\n552 |         The predictions. Note if there are 2 classes, array is of shape\n553 |         (X.shape[0], 1).\n554 |     \"\"\"\n555 |     predictions = pred_method(X=X)\n556 | \n557 |     if method_name == \"decision_function\":\n558 |         if predictions.ndim == 1:\n559 |             predictions = predictions[:, np.newaxis]\n560 |     elif method_name == \"predict_proba\":\n561 |         if n_classes == 2:\n562 |             predictions = predictions[:, 1:]\n563 |     else:  # pragma: no cover\n564 |         # this branch should be unreachable.\n565 |         raise ValueError(f\"Invalid prediction method: {method_name}\")\n566 |     return predictions\n567 | \n568 | \n569 | def _fit_calibrator(clf, predictions, y, classes, method, sample_weight=None):\n570 |     \"\"\"Fit calibrator(s) and return a `_CalibratedClassifier`\n571 |     instance.\n572 | \n573 |     `n_classes` (i.e. `len(clf.classes_)`) calibrators are fitted.\n574 |     However, if `n_classes` equals 2, one calibrator is fitted.\n575 | \n576 |     Parameters\n577 |     ----------\n578 |     clf : estimator instance\n579 |         Fitted classifier.\n580 | \n581 |     predictions : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_classes) or (n_samples, 1) \\\n582 |                     when binary.\n583 |         Raw predictions returned by the un-calibrated base classifier.\n584 | \n585 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n586 |         The targets.\n587 | \n588 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n589 |         All the prediction classes.\n590 | \n591 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n592 |         The method to use for calibration.\n593 | \n594 |     sample_weight : ndarray, shape (n_samples,), default=None\n595 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n596 | \n597 |     Returns\n598 |     -------\n599 |     pipeline : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n600 |     \"\"\"\n601 |     Y = label_binarize(y, classes=classes)\n602 |     label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(classes)\n603 |     pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(clf.classes_)\n604 |     calibrators = []\n605 |     for class_idx, this_pred in zip(pos_class_indices, predictions.T):\n606 |         if method == \"isotonic\":\n607 |             calibrator = IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\")\n608 |         elif method == \"sigmoid\":\n609 |             calibrator = _SigmoidCalibration()\n610 |         else:\n611 |             raise ValueError(\n612 |                 f\"'method' should be one of: 'sigmoid' or 'isotonic'. Got {method}.\"\n613 |             )\n614 |         calibrator.fit(this_pred, Y[:, class_idx], sample_weight)\n615 |         calibrators.append(calibrator)\n616 | \n617 |     pipeline = _CalibratedClassifier(clf, calibrators, method=method, classes=classes)\n618 |     return pipeline\n619 | \n620 | \n621 | class _CalibratedClassifier:\n622 |     \"\"\"Pipeline-like chaining a fitted classifier and its fitted calibrators.\n623 | \n624 |     Parameters\n625 |     ----------\n626 |     base_estimator : estimator instance\n627 |         Fitted classifier.\n628 | \n629 |     calibrators : list of fitted estimator instances\n630 |         List of fitted calibrators (either 'IsotonicRegression' or\n631 |         '_SigmoidCalibration'). The number of calibrators equals the number of\n632 |         classes. However, if there are 2 classes, the list contains only one\n633 |         fitted calibrator.\n634 | \n635 |     classes : array-like of shape (n_classes,)\n636 |         All the prediction classes.\n637 | \n638 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}, default='sigmoid'\n639 |         The method to use for calibration. Can be 'sigmoid' which\n640 |         corresponds to Platt's method or 'isotonic' which is a\n641 |         non-parametric approach based on isotonic regression.\n642 | \n643 |     Attributes\n644 |     ----------\n645 |     calibrators_ : list of fitted estimator instances\n646 |         Same as `calibrators`. Exposed for backward-compatibility. Use\n647 |         `calibrators` instead.\n648 | \n649 |         .. deprecated:: 0.24\n650 |            `calibrators_` is deprecated from 0.24 and will be removed in\n651 |            1.1 (renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\n652 |     \"\"\"\n653 | \n654 |     def __init__(self, base_estimator, calibrators, *, classes, method=\"sigmoid\"):\n655 |         self.base_estimator = base_estimator\n656 |         self.calibrators = calibrators\n657 |         self.classes = classes\n658 |         self.method = method\n659 | \n660 |     # TODO: Remove in 1.1\n661 |     # mypy error: Decorated property not supported\n662 |     @deprecated(  # type: ignore\n663 |         \"`calibrators_` is deprecated in 0.24 and will be removed in 1.1\"\n664 |         \"(renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\"\n665 |     )\n666 |     @property\n667 |     def calibrators_(self):\n668 |         return self.calibrators\n669 | \n670 |     def predict_proba(self, X):\n671 |         \"\"\"Calculate calibrated probabilities.\n672 | \n673 |         Calculates classification calibrated probabilities\n674 |         for each class, in a one-vs-all manner, for `X`.\n675 | \n676 |         Parameters\n677 |         ----------\n678 |         X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n679 |             The sample data.\n680 | \n681 |         Returns\n682 |         -------\n683 |         proba : array, shape (n_samples, n_classes)\n684 |             The predicted probabilities. Can be exact zeros.\n685 |         \"\"\"\n686 |         n_classes = len(self.classes)\n687 |         pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(self.base_estimator)\n688 |         predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes)\n689 | \n690 |         label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(self.classes)\n691 |         pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(self.base_estimator.classes_)\n692 | \n693 |         proba = np.zeros((_num_samples(X), n_classes))\n694 |         for class_idx, this_pred, calibrator in zip(\n695 |             pos_class_indices, predictions.T, self.calibrators\n696 |         ):\n697 |             if n_classes == 2:\n698 |                 # When binary, `predictions` consists only of predictions for\n699 |                 # clf.classes_[1] but `pos_class_indices` = 0\n700 |                 class_idx += 1\n701 |             proba[:, class_idx] = calibrator.predict(this_pred)\n702 | \n703 |         # Normalize the probabilities\n704 |         if n_classes == 2:\n705 |             proba[:, 0] = 1.0 - proba[:, 1]\n706 |         else:\n707 |             denominator = np.sum(proba, axis=1)[:, np.newaxis]\n708 |             # In the edge case where for each class calibrator returns a null\n709 |             # probability for a given sample, use the uniform distribution\n710 |             # instead.\n711 |             uniform_proba = np.full_like(proba, 1 / n_classes)\n712 |             proba = np.divide(\n713 |                 proba, denominator, out=uniform_proba, where=denominator != 0\n714 |             )\n715 | \n716 |         # Deal with cases where the predicted probability minimally exceeds 1.0\n717 |         proba[(1.0 < proba) & (proba <= 1.0 + 1e-5)] = 1.0\n718 | \n719 |         return proba\n720 | \n721 | \n722 | def _sigmoid_calibration(predictions, y, sample_weight=None):\n723 |     \"\"\"Probability Calibration with sigmoid method (Platt 2000)\n724 | \n725 |     Parameters\n726 |     ----------\n727 |     predictions : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n728 |         The decision function or predict proba for the samples.\n729 | \n730 |     y : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n731 |         The targets.\n732 | \n733 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n734 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n735 | \n736 |     Returns\n737 |     -------\n738 |     a : float\n739 |         The slope.\n740 | \n741 |     b : float\n742 |         The intercept.\n743 | \n744 |     References\n745 |     ----------\n746 |     Platt, \"Probabilistic Outputs for Support Vector Machines\"\n747 |     \"\"\"\n748 |     predictions = column_or_1d(predictions)\n749 |     y = column_or_1d(y)\n750 | \n751 |     F = predictions  # F follows Platt's notations\n752 | \n753 |     # Bayesian priors (see Platt end of section 2.2)\n754 |     prior0 = float(np.sum(y <= 0))\n755 |     prior1 = y.shape[0] - prior0\n756 |     T = np.zeros(y.shape)\n757 |     T[y > 0] = (prior1 + 1.0) / (prior1 + 2.0)\n758 |     T[y <= 0] = 1.0 / (prior0 + 2.0)\n759 |     T1 = 1.0 - T\n760 | \n761 |     def objective(AB):\n762 |         # From Platt (beginning of Section 2.2)\n763 |         P = expit(-(AB[0] * F + AB[1]))\n764 |         loss = -(xlogy(T, P) + xlogy(T1, 1.0 - P))\n765 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n766 |             return (sample_weight * loss).sum()\n767 |         else:\n768 |             return loss.sum()\n769 | \n770 |     def grad(AB):\n771 |         # gradient of the objective function\n772 |         P = expit(-(AB[0] * F + AB[1]))\n773 |         TEP_minus_T1P = T - P\n774 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n775 |             TEP_minus_T1P *= sample_weight\n776 |         dA = np.dot(TEP_minus_T1P, F)\n777 |         dB = np.sum(TEP_minus_T1P)\n778 |         return np.array([dA, dB])\n779 | \n780 |     AB0 = np.array([0.0, log((prior0 + 1.0) / (prior1 + 1.0))])\n781 |     AB_ = fmin_bfgs(objective, AB0, fprime=grad, disp=False)\n782 |     return AB_[0], AB_[1]\n783 | \n784 | \n785 | class _SigmoidCalibration(RegressorMixin, BaseEstimator):\n786 |     \"\"\"Sigmoid regression model.\n787 | \n788 |     Attributes\n789 |     ----------\n790 |     a_ : float\n791 |         The slope.\n792 | \n793 |     b_ : float\n794 |         The intercept.\n795 |     \"\"\"\n796 | \n797 |     def fit(self, X, y, sample_weight=None):\n798 |         \"\"\"Fit the model using X, y as training data.\n799 | \n800 |         Parameters\n801 |         ----------\n802 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n803 |             Training data.\n804 | \n805 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n806 |             Training target.\n807 | \n808 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n809 |             Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n810 | \n811 |         Returns\n812 |         -------\n813 |         self : object\n814 |             Returns an instance of self.\n815 |         \"\"\"\n816 |         X = column_or_1d(X)\n817 |         y = column_or_1d(y)\n818 |         X, y = indexable(X, y)\n819 | \n820 |         self.a_, self.b_ = _sigmoid_calibration(X, y, sample_weight)\n821 |         return self\n822 | \n823 |     def predict(self, T):\n824 |         \"\"\"Predict new data by linear interpolation.\n825 | \n826 |         Parameters\n827 |         ----------\n828 |         T : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n829 |             Data to predict from.\n830 | \n831 |         Returns\n832 |         -------\n833 |         T_ : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n834 |             The predicted data.\n835 |         \"\"\"\n836 |         T = column_or_1d(T)\n837 |         return expit(-(self.a_ * T + self.b_))\n838 | \n839 | \n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n946 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "After analyzing the issue and the codebase, here are the relevant locations that need modification to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 840\nfunction: calibration_curve\n\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py\nline: 72\nfunction: plot_calibration_curve\n\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py\nline: 87\nfunction: plot_calibration_curve\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 1085\nfunction: check_matplotlib_support\n\nexamples/model_selection/plot_cv_indices.py\nline: 14\nfunction: plot_cv_indices\n```\n\nHere's why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `sklearn/calibration.py`: This is the main file containing the `calibration_curve` function that computes the calibration curve data. It will be modified to fix the ordering issue mentioned in the issue description.\n\n2. `examples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py`: This is an example implementation that demonstrates how to use the calibration curve functionality. The function `plot_calibration_curve` in this example currently handles both plotting and calibration calculations.\n\n3. `examples/model_selection/plot_cv_indices.py`: This example shows how to visualize cross-validation behavior, which will be relevant for understanding the visualization requirements.\n\n4. `sklearn/utils/__init__.py`: Contains the `check_matplotlib_support` function that verifies matplotlib dependencies, which will be needed for the plotting function.\n\nThese locations were selected based on:\n- Direct relation to the requested feature (calibration curve plotting)\n- Existing implementation patterns in the codebase\n- Dependencies that need to be considered (matplotlib support)\n- Related examples that might need updates\n\nThe most important modifications would be in `sklearn/calibration.py` where the order of return arguments needs to be fixed, and in the example implementation files where the plotting function needs to be updated to match the new function signature and behavior.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the issue, and check for any relevant references in other parts of the codebase that may not have appeared in the search results. If not, continue searching for additional locations related to the issue.\nVerify that you have carefully analyzed the impact of the found locations on the repository, especially their dependencies. If you think you have solved the task, rewrite your final answer into the required location-only format, output ONLY the location block, and then call `finish` to finish.\nIMPORTANT: YOU SHOULD NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\n"}, {"content": "Let me verify the completeness of the analysis by checking additional dependencies and related code.\n\nFirst, let's check if there are any other files that import or use the calibration curve functionality:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve', 'import calibration_curve']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:docstring:2\nexamples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py:impl:44\nexamples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py:impl\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:impl:4\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:plot_calibration_curve\nsklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve\nsklearn/calibration.py:CalibratedClassifierCV\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_multiclass.py:imports:6\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_multiclass.py:impl:36\nsklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:docstring:2\nexamples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py:impl:44\nexamples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py:impl\nsklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:impl:4\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:plot_calibration_curve\nsklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:docstring:2\nexamples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py:impl:44\nexamples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py:impl\nsklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:impl:4\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:plot_calibration_curve\nsklearn/calibration.py:CalibratedClassifierCV\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_multiclass.py:imports:6\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_multiclass.py:impl:36\n##Searching for term \"from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ======================================\n  3 | Probability calibration of classifiers\n  4 | ======================================\n  5 | \n  6 | When performing classification you often want to predict not only\n  7 | the class label, but also the associated probability. This probability\n  8 | gives you some kind of confidence on the prediction. However, not all\n  9 | classifiers provide well-calibrated probabilities, some being over-confident\n 10 | while others being under-confident. Thus, a separate calibration of predicted\n 11 | probabilities is often desirable as a postprocessing. This example illustrates\n 12 | two different methods for this calibration and evaluates the quality of the\n 13 | returned probabilities using Brier's score\n 14 | (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brier_score).\n 15 | \n 16 | Compared are the estimated probability using a Gaussian naive Bayes classifier\n 17 | without calibration, with a sigmoid calibration, and with a non-parametric\n 18 | isotonic calibration. One can observe that only the non-parametric model is\n 19 | able to provide a probability calibration that returns probabilities close\n 20 | to the expected 0.5 for most of the samples belonging to the middle\n 21 | cluster with heterogeneous labels. This results in a significantly improved\n 22 | Brier score.\n 23 | \"\"\"\n 24 | print(__doc__)\n 25 | \n 26 | # Author: Mathieu Blondel <mathieu@mblondel.org>\n 27 | #         Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n 28 | #         Balazs Kegl <balazs.kegl@gmail.com>\n 29 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n 30 | # License: BSD Style.\n 31 | \n 32 | import numpy as np\n 33 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 34 | from matplotlib import cm\n 35 | \n 36 | from sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n 37 | from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\n 38 | from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss\n 39 | from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\n 40 | from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 41 | \n 42 | \n 43 | n_samples = 50000\n 44 | n_bins = 3  # use 3 bins for calibration_curve as we have 3 clusters here\n 45 | \n 46 | # Generate 3 blobs with 2 classes where the second blob contains\n 47 | # half positive samples and half negative samples. Probability in this\n 48 | # blob is therefore 0.5.\n 49 | centers = [(-5, -5), (0, 0), (5, 5)]\n 50 | X, y = make_blobs(n_samples=n_samples, centers=centers, shuffle=False,\n 51 |                   random_state=42)\n 52 | \n 53 | y[:n_samples // 2] = 0\n 54 | y[n_samples // 2:] = 1\n 55 | sample_weight = np.random.RandomState(42).rand(y.shape[0])\n 56 | \n 57 | # split train, test for calibration\n 58 | X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, sw_train, sw_test = \\\n 59 |     train_test_split(X, y, sample_weight, test_size=0.9, random_state=42)\n 60 | \n 61 | # Gaussian Naive-Bayes with no calibration\n 62 | clf = GaussianNB()\n 63 | clf.fit(X_train, y_train)  # GaussianNB itself does not support sample-weights\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==================================================\n  3 | Probability Calibration for 3-class classification\n  4 | ==================================================\n  5 | \n  6 | This example illustrates how sigmoid :ref:`calibration <calibration>` changes\n  7 | predicted probabilities for a 3-class classification problem. Illustrated is\n  8 | the standard 2-simplex, where the three corners correspond to the three\n  9 | classes. Arrows point from the probability vectors predicted by an uncalibrated\n 10 | classifier to the probability vectors predicted by the same classifier after\n 11 | sigmoid calibration on a hold-out validation set. Colors indicate the true\n 12 | class of an instance (red: class 1, green: class 2, blue: class 3).\n 13 | \"\"\"\n 14 | \n 15 | # %%\n 16 | # Data\n 17 | # ----\n 18 | # Below, we generate a classification dataset with 2000 samples, 2 features\n 19 | # and 3 target classes. We then split the data as follows:\n 20 | #\n 21 | # * train: 600 samples (for training the classifier)\n 22 | # * valid: 400 samples (for calibrating predicted probabilities)\n 23 | # * test: 1000 samples\n 24 | #\n 25 | # Note that we also create `X_train_valid` and `y_train_valid`, which consists\n 26 | # of both the train and valid subsets. This is used when we only want to train\n 27 | # the classifier but not calibrate the predicted probabilities.\n 28 | \n 29 | # Author: Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n 30 | # License: BSD Style.\n 31 | \n 32 | import numpy as np\n 33 | from sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n 34 | \n 35 | np.random.seed(0)\n 36 | \n 37 | X, y = make_blobs(n_samples=2000, n_features=2, centers=3, random_state=42,\n 38 |                   cluster_std=5.0)\n 39 | X_train, y_train = X[:600], y[:600]\n 40 | X_valid, y_valid = X[600:1000], y[600:1000]\n 41 | X_train_valid, y_train_valid = X[:1000], y[:1000]\n 42 | X_test, y_test = X[1000:], y[1000:]\n 43 | \n 44 | # %%\n 45 | # Fitting and calibration\n 46 | # -----------------------\n 47 | #\n 48 | # First, we will train a :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestClassifier`\n 49 | # with 25 base estimators (trees) on the concatenated train and validation\n 50 | # data (1000 samples). This is the uncalibrated classifier.\n 51 | \n 52 | from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n 53 | \n 54 | clf = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=25)\n 55 | clf.fit(X_train_valid, y_train_valid)\n 56 | \n 57 | # %%\n 58 | # To train the calibrated classifier, we start with the same\n 59 | # :class:`~sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestClassifier` but train it using only\n 60 | # the train data subset (600 samples) then calibrate, with `method='sigmoid'`,\n 61 | # using the valid data subset (400 samples) in a 2-stage process.\n 62 | \n 63 | from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\", \"import calibration_curve\", \"from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve import calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:calibration_curve`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n840 | def calibration_curve(y_true, y_prob, *, normalize=False, n_bins=5, strategy=\"uniform\"):\n841 |     \"\"\"Compute true and predicted probabilities for a calibration curve.\n842 | \n843 |     The method assumes the inputs come from a binary classifier, and\n844 |     discretize the [0, 1] interval into bins.\n845 | \n846 |     Calibration curves may also be referred to as reliability diagrams.\n847 | \n848 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <calibration>`.\n849 | \n850 |     Parameters\n851 |     ----------\n852 |     y_true : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n853 |         True targets.\n854 | \n855 |     y_prob : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n856 |         Probabilities of the positive class.\n857 | \n858 |     normalize : bool, default=False\n859 |         Whether y_prob needs to be normalized into the [0, 1] interval, i.e.\n860 |         is not a proper probability. If True, the smallest value in y_prob\n861 |         is linearly mapped onto 0 and the largest one onto 1.\n862 | \n863 |     n_bins : int, default=5\n864 |         Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n865 |         requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n866 |         corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n867 |         returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n868 | \n869 |     strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n870 |         Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n871 | \n872 |         uniform\n873 |             The bins have identical widths.\n874 |         quantile\n875 |             The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n876 | \n877 |     Returns\n878 |     -------\n879 |     prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n880 |         The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n881 |         bin (fraction of positives).\n882 | \n883 |     prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n884 |         The mean predicted probability in each bin.\n885 | \n886 |     References\n887 |     ----------\n888 |     Alexandru Niculescu-Mizil and Rich Caruana (2005) Predicting Good\n889 |     Probabilities With Supervised Learning, in Proceedings of the 22nd\n890 |     International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML).\n891 |     See section 4 (Qualitative Analysis of Predictions).\n892 | \n893 |     Examples\n894 |     --------\n895 |     >>> import numpy as np\n896 |     >>> from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\n897 |     >>> y_true = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1])\n898 |     >>> y_pred = np.array([0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.65, 0.7, 0.8, 0.9,  1.])\n899 |     >>> prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_true, y_pred, n_bins=3)\n900 |     >>> prob_true\n901 |     array([0. , 0.5, 1. ])\n902 |     >>> prob_pred\n903 |     array([0.2  , 0.525, 0.85 ])\n904 |     \"\"\"\n905 |     y_true = column_or_1d(y_true)\n906 |     y_prob = column_or_1d(y_prob)\n907 |     check_consistent_length(y_true, y_prob)\n908 | \n909 |     if normalize:  # Normalize predicted values into interval [0, 1]\n910 |         y_prob = (y_prob - y_prob.min()) / (y_prob.max() - y_prob.min())\n911 |     elif y_prob.min() < 0 or y_prob.max() > 1:\n912 |         raise ValueError(\n913 |             \"y_prob has values outside [0, 1] and normalize is set to False.\"\n914 |         )\n915 | \n916 |     labels = np.unique(y_true)\n917 |     if len(labels) > 2:\n918 |         raise ValueError(\n919 |             \"Only binary classification is supported. Provided labels %s.\" % labels\n920 |         )\n921 |     y_true = label_binarize(y_true, classes=labels)[:, 0]\n922 | \n923 |     if strategy == \"quantile\":  # Determine bin edges by distribution of data\n924 |         quantiles = np.linspace(0, 1, n_bins + 1)\n925 |         bins = np.percentile(y_prob, quantiles * 100)\n926 |         bins[-1] = bins[-1] + 1e-8\n927 |     elif strategy == \"uniform\":\n928 |         bins = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0 + 1e-8, n_bins + 1)\n929 |     else:\n930 |         raise ValueError(\n931 |             \"Invalid entry to 'strategy' input. Strategy \"\n932 |             \"must be either 'quantile' or 'uniform'.\"\n933 |         )\n934 | \n935 |     binids = np.digitize(y_prob, bins) - 1\n936 | \n937 |     bin_sums = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_prob, minlength=len(bins))\n938 |     bin_true = np.bincount(binids, weights=y_true, minlength=len(bins))\n939 |     bin_total = np.bincount(binids, minlength=len(bins))\n940 | \n941 |     nonzero = bin_total != 0\n942 |     prob_true = bin_true[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n943 |     prob_pred = bin_sums[nonzero] / bin_total[nonzero]\n944 | \n945 |     return prob_true, prob_pred\n```\n\nFound function `examples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:plot_calibration_curve`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 72 | def plot_calibration_curve(est, name, fig_index):\n 73 |     \"\"\"Plot calibration curve for est w/o and with calibration. \"\"\"\n 74 |     # Calibrated with isotonic calibration\n 75 |     isotonic = CalibratedClassifierCV(est, cv=2, method='isotonic')\n 76 | \n 77 |     # Calibrated with sigmoid calibration\n 78 |     sigmoid = CalibratedClassifierCV(est, cv=2, method='sigmoid')\n 79 | \n 80 |     # Logistic regression with no calibration as baseline\n 81 |     lr = LogisticRegression(C=1.)\n 82 | \n 83 |     fig = plt.figure(fig_index, figsize=(10, 10))\n 84 |     ax1 = plt.subplot2grid((3, 1), (0, 0), rowspan=2, fig=fig)\n 85 |     ax2 = plt.subplot2grid((3, 1), (2, 0), fig=fig)\n 86 | \n 87 |     ax1.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], \"k:\", label=\"Perfectly calibrated\")\n 88 |     for clf, name in [(lr, 'Logistic'),\n 89 |                       (est, name),\n 90 |                       (isotonic, name + ' + Isotonic'),\n 91 |                       (sigmoid, name + ' + Sigmoid')]:\n 92 |         clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 93 |         y_pred = clf.predict(X_test)\n 94 |         if hasattr(clf, \"predict_proba\"):\n 95 |             prob_pos = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n 96 |         else:  # use decision function\n 97 |             prob_pos = clf.decision_function(X_test)\n 98 |             prob_pos = \\\n 99 |                 (prob_pos - prob_pos.min()) / (prob_pos.max() - prob_pos.min())\n100 | \n101 |         clf_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos, pos_label=y.max())\n102 |         print(\"%s:\" % name)\n103 |         print(\"\\tBrier: %1.3f\" % (clf_score))\n104 |         print(\"\\tPrecision: %1.3f\" % precision_score(y_test, y_pred))\n105 |         print(\"\\tRecall: %1.3f\" % recall_score(y_test, y_pred))\n106 |         print(\"\\tF1: %1.3f\\n\" % f1_score(y_test, y_pred))\n107 | \n108 |         fraction_of_positives, mean_predicted_value = \\\n109 |             calibration_curve(y_test, prob_pos, n_bins=10)\n110 | \n111 |         ax1.plot(mean_predicted_value, fraction_of_positives, \"s-\",\n112 |                  label=\"%s (%1.3f)\" % (name, clf_score))\n113 | \n114 |         ax2.hist(prob_pos, range=(0, 1), bins=10, label=name,\n115 |                  histtype=\"step\", lw=2)\n116 | \n117 |     ax1.set_ylabel(\"Fraction of positives\")\n118 |     ax1.set_ylim([-0.05, 1.05])\n119 |     ax1.legend(loc=\"lower right\")\n120 |     ax1.set_title('Calibration plots  (reliability curve)')\n121 | \n122 |     ax2.set_xlabel(\"Mean predicted value\")\n123 |     ax2.set_ylabel(\"Count\")\n124 |     ax2.legend(loc=\"upper center\", ncol=2)\n125 | \n126 |     plt.tight_layout()\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve\", \"from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve import calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:_compute_predictions`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n531 | def _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes):\n532 |     \"\"\"Return predictions for `X` and reshape binary outputs to shape\n533 |     (n_samples, 1).\n534 | \n535 |     Parameters\n536 |     ----------\n537 |     pred_method : callable\n538 |         Prediction method.\n539 | \n540 |     method_name: str\n541 |         Name of the prediction method\n542 | \n543 |     X : array-like or None\n544 |         Data used to obtain predictions.\n545 | \n546 |     n_classes : int\n547 |         Number of classes present.\n548 | \n549 |     Returns\n550 |     -------\n551 |     predictions : array-like, shape (X.shape[0], len(clf.classes_))\n552 |         The predictions. Note if there are 2 classes, array is of shape\n553 |         (X.shape[0], 1).\n554 |     \"\"\"\n555 |     predictions = pred_method(X=X)\n556 | \n557 |     if method_name == \"decision_function\":\n558 |         if predictions.ndim == 1:\n559 |             predictions = predictions[:, np.newaxis]\n560 |     elif method_name == \"predict_proba\":\n561 |         if n_classes == 2:\n562 |             predictions = predictions[:, 1:]\n563 |     else:  # pragma: no cover\n564 |         # this branch should be unreachable.\n565 |         raise ValueError(f\"Invalid prediction method: {method_name}\")\n566 |     return predictions\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:_fit_calibrator`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n569 | def _fit_calibrator(clf, predictions, y, classes, method, sample_weight=None):\n570 |     \"\"\"Fit calibrator(s) and return a `_CalibratedClassifier`\n571 |     instance.\n572 | \n573 |     `n_classes` (i.e. `len(clf.classes_)`) calibrators are fitted.\n574 |     However, if `n_classes` equals 2, one calibrator is fitted.\n575 | \n576 |     Parameters\n577 |     ----------\n578 |     clf : estimator instance\n579 |         Fitted classifier.\n580 | \n581 |     predictions : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_classes) or (n_samples, 1) \\\n582 |                     when binary.\n583 |         Raw predictions returned by the un-calibrated base classifier.\n584 | \n585 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n586 |         The targets.\n587 | \n588 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n589 |         All the prediction classes.\n590 | \n591 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n592 |         The method to use for calibration.\n593 | \n594 |     sample_weight : ndarray, shape (n_samples,), default=None\n595 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n596 | \n597 |     Returns\n598 |     -------\n599 |     pipeline : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n600 |     \"\"\"\n601 |     Y = label_binarize(y, classes=classes)\n602 |     label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(classes)\n603 |     pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(clf.classes_)\n604 |     calibrators = []\n605 |     for class_idx, this_pred in zip(pos_class_indices, predictions.T):\n606 |         if method == \"isotonic\":\n607 |             calibrator = IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\")\n608 |         elif method == \"sigmoid\":\n609 |             calibrator = _SigmoidCalibration()\n610 |         else:\n611 |             raise ValueError(\n612 |                 f\"'method' should be one of: 'sigmoid' or 'isotonic'. Got {method}.\"\n613 |             )\n614 |         calibrator.fit(this_pred, Y[:, class_idx], sample_weight)\n615 |         calibrators.append(calibrator)\n616 | \n617 |     pipeline = _CalibratedClassifier(clf, calibrators, method=method, classes=classes)\n618 |     return pipeline\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/calibration.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/calibration.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"import calibration_curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ======================================\n  3 | Probability calibration of classifiers\n  4 | ======================================\n  5 | \n  6 | When performing classification you often want to predict not only\n  7 | the class label, but also the associated probability. This probability\n  8 | gives you some kind of confidence on the prediction. However, not all\n  9 | classifiers provide well-calibrated probabilities, some being over-confident\n 10 | while others being under-confident. Thus, a separate calibration of predicted\n 11 | probabilities is often desirable as a postprocessing. This example illustrates\n 12 | two different methods for this calibration and evaluates the quality of the\n 13 | returned probabilities using Brier's score\n 14 | (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brier_score).\n 15 | \n 16 | Compared are the estimated probability using a Gaussian naive Bayes classifier\n 17 | without calibration, with a sigmoid calibration, and with a non-parametric\n 18 | isotonic calibration. One can observe that only the non-parametric model is\n 19 | able to provide a probability calibration that returns probabilities close\n 20 | to the expected 0.5 for most of the samples belonging to the middle\n 21 | cluster with heterogeneous labels. This results in a significantly improved\n 22 | Brier score.\n 23 | \"\"\"\n 24 | print(__doc__)\n 25 | \n 26 | # Author: Mathieu Blondel <mathieu@mblondel.org>\n 27 | #         Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n 28 | #         Balazs Kegl <balazs.kegl@gmail.com>\n 29 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n 30 | # License: BSD Style.\n 31 | \n 32 | import numpy as np\n 33 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 34 | from matplotlib import cm\n 35 | \n 36 | from sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n 37 | from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\n 38 | from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss\n 39 | from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\n 40 | from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 41 | \n 42 | \n 43 | n_samples = 50000\n 44 | n_bins = 3  # use 3 bins for calibration_curve as we have 3 clusters here\n 45 | \n 46 | # Generate 3 blobs with 2 classes where the second blob contains\n 47 | # half positive samples and half negative samples. Probability in this\n 48 | # blob is therefore 0.5.\n 49 | centers = [(-5, -5), (0, 0), (5, 5)]\n 50 | X, y = make_blobs(n_samples=n_samples, centers=centers, shuffle=False,\n 51 |                   random_state=42)\n 52 | \n 53 | y[:n_samples // 2] = 0\n 54 | y[n_samples // 2:] = 1\n 55 | sample_weight = np.random.RandomState(42).rand(y.shape[0])\n 56 | \n 57 | # split train, test for calibration\n 58 | X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, sw_train, sw_test = \\\n 59 |     train_test_split(X, y, sample_weight, test_size=0.9, random_state=42)\n 60 | \n 61 | # Gaussian Naive-Bayes with no calibration\n 62 | clf = GaussianNB()\n 63 | clf.fit(X_train, y_train)  # GaussianNB itself does not support sample-weights\n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/metrics/__init__.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | The :mod:`sklearn.metrics` module includes score functions, performance metrics\n  3 | and pairwise metrics and distance computations.\n  4 | \"\"\"\n  5 | \n  6 | \n  7 | from ._ranking import auc\n  8 | from ._ranking import average_precision_score\n  9 | from ._ranking import coverage_error\n 10 | from ._ranking import det_curve\n 11 | from ._ranking import dcg_score\n 12 | from ._ranking import label_ranking_average_precision_score\n 13 | from ._ranking import label_ranking_loss\n 14 | from ._ranking import ndcg_score\n 15 | from ._ranking import precision_recall_curve\n 16 | from ._ranking import roc_auc_score\n 17 | from ._ranking import roc_curve\n 18 | from ._ranking import top_k_accuracy_score\n 19 | \n 20 | from ._classification import accuracy_score\n 21 | from ._classification import balanced_accuracy_score\n 22 | from ._classification import classification_report\n 23 | from ._classification import cohen_kappa_score\n 24 | from ._classifi