e multi-class\n147 |         classification. Note that all classifiers support binary\n148 |         classification. Therefore this flag indicates whether the\n149 |         classifier is a binary-classifier-only or not.\n150 | \n151 |     multi_label : bool, default=False\n152 |         Whether the classifier supports multi-label output.\n153 |     \"\"\"\n154 | \n155 |     poor_score: bool = False\n156 |     multi_class: bool = True\n157 |     multi_label: bool = False\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/utils/_tags.py:Tags`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n183 | class Tags:\n184 |     \"\"\"Tags for the estimator.\n185 | \n186 |     See :ref:`estimator_tags` for more information.\n187 | \n188 |     Parameters\n189 |     ----------\n190 |     estimator_type : str or None\n191 |         The type of the estimator. Can be one of:\n192 |         - \"classifier\"\n193 |         - \"regressor\"\n194 |         - \"transformer\"\n195 |         - \"clusterer\"\n196 |         - \"outlier_detector\"\n197 |         - \"density_estimator\"\n198 | \n199 |     target_tags : :class:`TargetTags`\n200 |         The target(y) tags.\n201 | \n202 |     transformer_tags : :class:`TransformerTags` or None\n203 |         The transformer tags.\n204 | \n205 |     classifier_tags : :class:`ClassifierTags` or None\n206 |         The classifier tags.\n207 | \n208 |     regressor_tags : :class:`RegressorTags` or None\n209 |         The regressor tags.\n210 | \n211 |     array_api_support : bool, default=False\n212 |         Whether the estimator supports Array API compatible inputs.\n213 | \n214 |     no_validation : bool, default=False\n215 |         Whether the estimator skips input-validation. This is only meant for\n216 |         stateless and dummy transformers!\n217 | \n218 |     non_deterministic : bool, default=False\n219 |         Whether the estimator is not deterministic given a fixed ``random_state``.\n220 | \n221 |     requires_fit : bool, default=True\n222 |         Whether the estimator requires to be fitted before calling one of\n223 |         `transform`, `predict`, `predict_proba`, or `decision_function`.\n224 | \n225 |     _skip_test : bool, default=False\n226 |         Whether to skip common tests entirely. Don't use this unless\n227 |         you have a *very good* reason.\n228 | \n229 |     input_tags : :class:`InputTags`\n230 |         The input data(X) tags.\n231 |     \"\"\"\n232 | \n233 |     estimator_type: str | None\n234 |     target_tags: TargetTags\n235 |     transformer_tags: TransformerTags | None = None\n236 |     classifier_tags: ClassifierTags | None = None\n237 |     regressor_tags: RegressorTags | None = None\n238 |     array_api_support: bool = False\n239 |     no_validation: bool = False\n240 |     non_deterministic: bool = False\n241 |     requires_fit: bool = True\n242 |     _skip_test: bool = False\n243 |     input_tags: InputTags = field(default_factory=InputTags)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n247 | def default_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n248 |     \"\"\"Get the default tags for an estimator.\n249 | \n250 |     This ignores any ``__sklearn_tags__`` method that the estimator may have.\n251 | \n252 |     If the estimator is a classifier or a regressor, ``target_tags.required``\n253 |     will be set to ``True``, otherwise it will be set to ``False``.\n254 | \n255 |     ``transformer_tags`` will be set to :class:`~.sklearn.utils. TransformerTags` if the\n256 |     estimator has a ``transform`` or ``fit_transform`` method, otherwise it will be set\n257 |     to ``None``.\n258 | \n259 |     ``classifier_tags`` will be set to :class:`~.sklearn.utils.ClassifierTags` if the\n260 |     estimator is a classifier, otherwise it will be set to ``None``.\n261 |     a classifier, otherwise it will be set to ``None``.\n262 | \n263 |     ``regressor_tags`` will be set to :class:`~.sklearn.utils.RegressorTags` if the\n264 |     estimator is a regressor, otherwise it will be set to ``None``.\n265 | \n266 |     Parameters\n267 |     ----------\n268 |     estimator : estimator object\n269 |         The estimator for which to get the default tags.\n270 | \n271 |     Returns\n272 |     -------\n273 |     tags : Tags\n274 |         The default tags for the estimator.\n275 |     \"\"\"\n276 |     est_is_classifier = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None) == \"classifier\"\n277 |     est_is_regressor = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None) == \"regressor\"\n278 |     target_required = est_is_classifier or est_is_regressor\n279 | \n280 |     return Tags(\n281 |         estimator_type=getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None),\n282 |         target_tags=TargetTags(required=target_required),\n283 |         transformer_tags=(\n284 |             TransformerTags()\n285 |             if hasattr(estimator, \"transform\") or hasattr(estimator, \"fit_transform\")\n286 |             else None\n287 |         ),\n288 |         classifier_tags=ClassifierTags() if est_is_classifier else None,\n289 |         regressor_tags=RegressorTags() if est_is_regressor else None,\n290 |     )\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"ClassifierTags\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/utils/__init__.py:__getattr__`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n106 | def __getattr__(name):\n107 |     if name == \"IS_PYPY\":\n108 |         warnings.warn(\n109 |             \"IS_PYPY is deprecated and will be removed in 1.7.\",\n110 |             FutureWarning,\n111 |         )\n112 |         return platform.python_implementation() == \"PyPy\"\n113 |     raise AttributeError(f\"module {__name__} has no attribute {name}\")\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"RegressorTags\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/utils/_tags.py:RegressorTags`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `RegressorTags`.\n```\n161 | class RegressorTags:\n162 |     \"\"\"Tags for the regressor.\n163 | \n164 |     Parameters\n165 |     ----------\n166 |     poor_score : bool, default=False\n167 |         Whether the estimator fails to provide a \"reasonable\" test-set\n168 |         score, which currently for regression is an R2 of 0.5 on\n169 |         ``make_regression(n_samples=200, n_features=10,\n170 |         n_informative=1, bias=5.0, noise=20, random_state=42)``. The\n171 |         dataset and values are based on current estimators in scikit-learn\n172 |         and might be replaced by something more systematic.\n173 | \n174 |     multi_label : bool, default=False\n175 |         Whether the regressor supports multilabel output.\n176 |     \"\"\"\n177 | \n178 |     poor_score: bool = False\n179 |     multi_label: bool = False\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"target_tags ClassifierTags\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/base.py:ClassifierMixin`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n475 | class ClassifierMixin:\n476 |     \"\"\"Mixin class for all classifiers in scikit-learn.\n477 | \n478 |     This mixin defines the following functionality:\n479 | \n480 |     - set estimator type to `\"classifier\"` through the `estimator_type` tag;\n481 |     - `score` method that default to :func:`~sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score`.\n482 |     - enforce that `fit` requires `y` to be passed through the `requires_y` tag,\n483 |       which is done by setting the classifier type tag.\n484 | \n485 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <rolling_your_own_estimator>`.\n486 | \n487 |     Examples\n488 |     --------\n489 |     >>> import numpy as np\n490 |     >>> from sklearn.base import BaseEstimator, ClassifierMixin\n491 |     >>> # Mixin classes should always be on the left-hand side for a correct MRO\n492 |     >>> class MyEstimator(ClassifierMixin, BaseEstimator):\n493 |     ...     def __init__(self, *, param=1):\n494 |     ...         self.param = param\n495 |     ...     def fit(self, X, y=None):\n496 |     ...         self.is_fitted_ = True\n497 |     ...         return self\n498 |     ...     def predict(self, X):\n499 |     ...         return np.full(shape=X.shape[0], fill_value=self.param)\n500 |     >>> estimator = MyEstimator(param=1)\n501 |     >>> X = np.array([[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]])\n502 |     >>> y = np.array([1, 0, 1])\n503 |     >>> estimator.fit(X, y).predict(X)\n504 |     array([1, 1, 1])\n505 |     >>> estimator.score(X, y)\n506 |     0.66...\n507 |     \"\"\"\n508 | \n509 |     # TODO(1.8): Remove this attribute\n510 |     _estimator_type = \"classifier\"\n511 | \n512 |     def __sklearn_tags__(self):\n513 |         tags = super().__sklearn_tags__()\n514 |         tags.estimator_type = \"classifier\"\n515 |         tags.classifier_tags = ClassifierTags()\n516 |         tags.target_tags.required = True\n517 |         return tags\n518 | \n519 |     def score(self, X, y, sample_weight=None):\n520 |         \"\"\"\n521 |         Return the mean accuracy on the given test data and labels.\n522 | \n523 |         In multi-label classification, this is the subset accuracy\n524 |         which is a harsh metric since you require for each sample that\n525 |         each label set be correctly predicted.\n526 | \n527 |         Parameters\n528 |         ----------\n529 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n530 |             Test samples.\n531 | \n532 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_outputs)\n533 |             True labels for `X`.\n534 | \n535 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n536 |             Sample weights.\n537 | \n538 |         Returns\n539 |         -------\n540 |         score : float\n541 |             Mean accuracy of ``self.predict(X)`` w.r.t. `y`.\n542 |         \"\"\"\n543 |         from .metrics import accuracy_score\n544 | \n545 |         return accuracy_score(y, self.predict(X), sample_weight=sample_weight)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of the issue and the codebase, I can now identify the key locations that need to be modified to implement the tag conversion feature. Here's the result in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 293-334\nfunction: get_tags\ndescription: Main function that needs modification to support tag conversion\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nline: 4447-4466\nfunction: check_estimator_tags_renamed\ndescription: Validation logic for tags implementation\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 512-517\nclass: ClassifierMixin\nfunction: __sklearn_tags__\ndescription: Implementation showing how to properly define tags\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 247-290\nfunction: default_tags\ndescription: Helper function for tag initialization\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 106-113\nfunction: __getattr__\ndescription: Reference for handling deprecation warnings\n```\n\nThese locations cover:\n1. The main tag conversion logic in `get_tags`\n2. The tag renaming validation in `check_estimator_tags_renamed`\n3. The proper implementation pattern in `ClassifierMixin`\n4. The default tags initialization\n5. The deprecation handling utilities\n\nThe core changes will focus on modifying the `get_tags` function to properly handle tag conversion while maintaining backward compatibility. The implementation needs to detect when an estimator is using the old tags system versus the new system, and warn appropriately when necessary.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 82937, "completion_tokens": 544}, "time": 86.25029921531677}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-28678", "found_files": [["sklearn/utils/__init__.py", "doc/sphinxext/autoshortsummary.py", "sklearn/base.py", "sklearn/utils/validation.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:LegacyVersion", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/decomposition.py:PCABenchmark", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/svm.py:SVCBenchmark", "benchmarks/bench_glmnet.py:rmse", "doc/sphinxext/autoshortsummary.py:ShortSummaryDocumenter", "sklearn/feature_extraction/_hash.py:FeatureHasher", "examples/release_highlights/plot_release_highlights_1_5_0.py:smallest_abs", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:LegacyVersion.__str__", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/decomposition.py:PCABenchmark.setup_cache", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/svm.py:SVCBenchmark.make_scorers", "benchmarks/bench_glmnet.py:rmse", "doc/sphinxext/autoshortsummary.py:ShortSummaryDocumenter.add_content", "sklearn/feature_extraction/_hash.py:FeatureHasher._more_tags", "examples/release_highlights/plot_release_highlights_1_5_0.py:smallest_abs", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator._get_param_names"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the code exploration, I've analyzed the issue and can now provide the locations that need to be modified to implement the `assert_consistent_docs()` feature. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/doc_validation.py\nline: 1\nfunction: assert_consistent_docs\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 15\nline: 16\nline: 17\n\ndoc/sphinxext/autoshortsummary.py\nline: 30\nline: 31\nclass: ShortSummaryDocumenter\nfunction: add_content\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 197\nline: 198\nline: 199\nfunction: BaseEstimator._get_param_names\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 148\nline: 149\nfunction: validate_params\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `sklearn/utils/doc_validation.py`: This is where the main `assert_consistent_docs()` function needs to be implemented. It should handle the logic for checking docstring consistency across parameters, attributes, and returns sections.\n\n2. `sklearn/utils/__init__.py`: This file needs to expose the new function through the package's public interface, similar to how other utility functions are exposed.\n\n3. `doc/sphinxext/autoshortsummary.py`: The `ShortSummaryDocumenter` class in this file processes docstrings for Sphinx documentation generation. It's relevant as it handles the basic docstring parsing and might need integration with the new validation function.\n\n4. `sklearn/base.py`: The `BaseEstimator._get_param_names()` function is used to get parameter names. This will be crucial for comparing docstrings across different estimators.\n\n5. `sklearn/utils/validation.py`: The existing `validate_params()` function shows how parameter validation patterns are handled, which will be important for integrating into the new docstring consistency check.\n\nThese locations form the core components needed to implement the docstring consistency checker. The main function would need to:\n1. Parse docstrings from each component\n2. Compare them against reference docstrings\n3. Track missing parameters/attributes/returns\n4. Provide clear error messages for inconsistencies"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "2cbf3345bce979b261f7007616cc3465f33a51d3", "problem_statement": "[WIP] : Added assert_consistent_docs() and related tests\n\r\nFixes #9388 \r\n\r\nAdded a function to check for consistency between docstring of objects.\r\n\r\nIn this approach there is a python dictionary for each of Parameters, Attributes and Returns. Indexed by the name of the parameter/attribute/return with the value being a Python dictionary containing its type definition and description. The function checks for each object if the docstring is identical (excluding whitespaces) for a given parameter/attribute/return in the main dictionary. If a new parameter/attribute/return are found they are added in the main dictionary.\n", "patch": "diff --git a/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py b/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\nindex 0175966a21b74..b69855633a730 100644\n--- a/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\n+++ b/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\n@@ -1727,14 +1727,18 @@ def precision_recall_fscore_support(\n         \"assigned\" 0 samples. For multilabel targets, labels are column indices.\n         By default, all labels in `y_true` and `y_pred` are used in sorted order.\n \n+        .. versionchanged:: 0.17\n+           Parameter `labels` improved for multiclass problem.\n+\n     pos_label : int, float, bool or str, default=1\n         The class to report if `average='binary'` and the data is binary,\n         otherwise this parameter is ignored.\n         For multiclass or multilabel targets, set `labels=[pos_label]` and\n         `average != 'binary'` to report metrics for one label only.\n \n-    average : {'binary', 'micro', 'macro', 'samples', 'weighted'}, \\\n-            default=None\n+    average : {'micro', 'macro', 'samples', 'weighted', 'binary'} or None, \\\n+            default='binary'\n+        This parameter is required for multiclass/multilabel targets.\n         If ``None``, the metrics for each class are returned. Otherwise, this\n         determines the type of averaging performed on the data:\n \n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 122017, "completion_tokens": 736}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class