sions\n2007 |         that will be transformed. If it is not passed, all possible expressions\n2008 |         will be rewritten. *rule* defines how the expression will be rewritten.\n2009 | \n2010 |         Parameters\n2011 |         ==========\n2012 | \n2013 |         args : Expr\n2014 |             A *rule*, or *pattern* and *rule*.\n2015 |             - *pattern* is a type or an iterable of types.\n2016 |             - *rule* can be any object.\n2017 | \n2018 |         deep : bool, optional\n2019 |             If ``True``, subexpressions are recursively transformed. Default is\n2020 |             ``True``.\n2021 | \n2022 |         Examples\n2023 |         ========\n2024 | \n2025 |         If *pattern* is unspecified, all possible expressions are transformed.\n2026 | \n2027 |         >>> from sympy import cos, sin, exp, I\n2028 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x\n2029 |         >>> expr = cos(x) + I*sin(x)\n2030 |         >>> expr.rewrite(exp)\n2031 |         exp(I*x)\n2032 | \n2033 |         Pattern can be a type or an iterable of types.\n2034 | \n2035 |         >>> expr.rewrite(sin, exp)\n2036 |         exp(I*x)/2 + cos(x) - exp(-I*x)/2\n2037 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos,], exp)\n2038 |         exp(I*x)/2 + I*sin(x) + exp(-I*x)/2\n2039 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos, sin], exp)\n2040 |         exp(I*x)\n2041 | \n2042 |         Rewriting behavior can be implemented by defining ``_eval_rewrite()``\n2043 |         method.\n2044 | \n2045 |         >>> from sympy import Expr, sqrt, pi\n2046 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2047 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite(self, rule, args, **hints):\n2048 |         ...         x, = args\n2049 |         ...         if rule == cos:\n2050 |         ...             return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2051 |         ...         if rule == sqrt:\n2052 |         ...             return sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2053 |         >>> MySin(MySin(x)).rewrite(cos)\n2054 |         cos(-cos(-x + pi/2) + pi/2)\n2055 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(sqrt)\n2056 |         sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2057 | \n2058 |         Defining ``_eval_rewrite_as_[...]()`` method is supported for backwards\n2059 |         compatibility reason. This may be removed in the future and using it is\n2060 |         discouraged.\n2061 | \n2062 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2063 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite_as_cos(self, *args, **hints):\n2064 |         ...         x, = args\n2065 |         ...         return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2066 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(cos)\n2067 |         cos(-x + pi/2)\n2068 | \n2069 |         \"\"\"\n2070 |         if not args:\n2071 |             return self\n2072 | \n2073 |         hints.update(deep=deep)\n2074 | \n2075 |         pattern = args[:-1]\n2076 |         rule = args[-1]\n2077 | \n2078 |         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n2079 |         if isinstance(rule, str):\n2080 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n2081 |         elif hasattr(rule, \"__name__\"):\n2082 |             # rule is class or function\n2083 |             clsname = rule.__name__\n2084 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2085 |         else:\n2086 |             # rule is instance\n2087 |             clsname = rule.__class__.__name__\n2088 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2089 | \n2090 |         if pattern:\n2091 |             if iterable(pattern[0]):\n2092 |                 pattern = pattern[0]\n2093 |             pattern = tuple(p for p in pattern if self.has(p))\n2094 |             if not pattern:\n2095 |                 return self\n2096 |         # hereafter, empty pattern is interpreted as all pattern.\n2097 | \n2098 |         return self._rewrite(pattern, rule, method, **hints)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"rewrite(abs\", \"rewrite(Abs)\", \"rewrite(abs rewrite(Abs)\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sympy/solvers/solveset.py:solveset`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2337 | def solveset(f, symbol=None, domain=S.Complexes):\n2338 |     r\"\"\"Solves a given inequality or equation with set as output\n2339 | \n2340 |     Parameters\n2341 |     ==========\n2342 | \n2343 |     f : Expr or a relational.\n2344 |         The target equation or inequality\n2345 |     symbol : Symbol\n2346 |         The variable for which the equation is solved\n2347 |     domain : Set\n2348 |         The domain over which the equation is solved\n2349 | \n2350 |     Returns\n2351 |     =======\n2352 | \n2353 |     Set\n2354 |         A set of values for `symbol` for which `f` is True or is equal to\n2355 |         zero. An :class:`~.EmptySet` is returned if `f` is False or nonzero.\n2356 |         A :class:`~.ConditionSet` is returned as unsolved object if algorithms\n2357 |         to evaluate complete solution are not yet implemented.\n2358 | \n2359 |     ``solveset`` claims to be complete in the solution set that it returns.\n2360 | \n2361 |     Raises\n2362 |     ======\n2363 | \n2364 |     NotImplementedError\n2365 |         The algorithms to solve inequalities in complex domain  are\n2366 |         not yet implemented.\n2367 |     ValueError\n2368 |         The input is not valid.\n2369 |     RuntimeError\n2370 |         It is a bug, please report to the github issue tracker.\n2371 | \n2372 | \n2373 |     Notes\n2374 |     =====\n2375 | \n2376 |     Python interprets 0 and 1 as False and True, respectively, but\n2377 |     in this function they refer to solutions of an expression. So 0 and 1\n2378 |     return the domain and EmptySet, respectively, while True and False\n2379 |     return the opposite (as they are assumed to be solutions of relational\n2380 |     expressions).\n2381 | \n2382 | \n2383 |     See Also\n2384 |     ========\n2385 | \n2386 |     solveset_real: solver for real domain\n2387 |     solveset_complex: solver for complex domain\n2388 | \n2389 |     Examples\n2390 |     ========\n2391 | \n2392 |     >>> from sympy import exp, sin, Symbol, pprint, S, Eq\n2393 |     >>> from sympy.solvers.solveset import solveset, solveset_real\n2394 | \n2395 |     * The default domain is complex. Not specifying a domain will lead\n2396 |       to the solving of the equation in the complex domain (and this\n2397 |       is not affected by the assumptions on the symbol):\n2398 | \n2399 |     >>> x = Symbol('x')\n2400 |     >>> pprint(solveset(exp(x) - 1, x), use_unicode=False)\n2401 |     {2*n*I*pi | n in Integers}\n2402 | \n2403 |     >>> x = Symbol('x', real=True)\n2404 |     >>> pprint(solveset(exp(x) - 1, x), use_unicode=False)\n2405 |     {2*n*I*pi | n in Integers}\n2406 | \n2407 |     * If you want to use ``solveset`` to solve the equation in the\n2408 |       real domain, provide a real domain. (Using ``solveset_real``\n2409 |       does this automatically.)\n2410 | \n2411 |     >>> R = S.Reals\n2412 |     >>> x = Symbol('x')\n2413 |     >>> solveset(exp(x) - 1, x, R)\n2414 |     {0}\n2415 |     >>> solveset_real(exp(x) - 1, x)\n2416 |     {0}\n2417 | \n2418 |     The solution is unaffected by assumptions on the symbol:\n2419 | \n2420 |     >>> p = Symbol('p', positive=True)\n2421 |     >>> pprint(solveset(p**2 - 4))\n2422 |     {-2, 2}\n2423 | \n2424 |     When a :class:`~.ConditionSet` is returned, symbols with assumptions that\n2425 |     would alter the set are replaced with more generic symbols:\n2426 | \n2427 |     >>> i = Symbol('i', imaginary=True)\n2428 |     >>> solveset(Eq(i**2 + i*sin(i), 1), i, domain=S.Reals)\n2429 |     ConditionSet(_R, Eq(_R**2 + _R*sin(_R) - 1, 0), Reals)\n2430 | \n2431 |     * Inequalities can be solved over the real domain only. Use of a complex\n2432 |       domain leads to a NotImplementedError.\n2433 | \n2434 |     >>> solveset(exp(x) > 1, x, R)\n2435 |     Interval.open(0, oo)\n2436 | \n2437 |     \"\"\"\n2438 |     f = sympify(f)\n2439 |     symbol = sympify(symbol)\n2440 | \n2441 |     if f is S.true:\n2442 |         return domain\n2443 | \n2444 |     if f is S.false:\n2445 |         return S.EmptySet\n2446 | \n2447 |     if not isinstance(f, (Expr, Relational, Number)):\n2448 |         raise ValueError(\"%s is not a valid SymPy expression\" % f)\n2449 | \n2450 |     if not isinstance(symbol, (Expr, Relational)) and  symbol is not None:\n2451 |         raise ValueError(\"%s is not a valid SymPy symbol\" % (symbol,))\n2452 | \n2453 |     if not isinstance(domain, Set):\n2454 |         raise ValueError(\"%s is not a valid domain\" %(domain))\n2455 | \n2456 |     free_symbols = f.free_symbols\n2457 | \n2458 |     if f.has(Piecewise):\n2459 |         f = piecewise_fold(f)\n2460 | \n2461 |     if symbol is None and not free_symbols:\n2462 |         b = Eq(f, 0)\n2463 |         if b is S.true:\n2464 |             return domain\n2465 |         elif b is S.false:\n2466 |             return S.EmptySet\n2467 |         else:\n2468 |             raise NotImplementedError(filldedent('''\n2469 |                 relationship between value and 0 is unknown: %s''' % b))\n2470 | \n2471 |     if symbol is None:\n2472 |         if len(free_symbols) == 1:\n2473 |             symbol = free_symbols.pop()\n2474 |         elif free_symbols:\n2475 |             raise ValueError(filldedent('''\n2476 |                 The independent variable must be specified for a\n2477 |                 multivariate equation.'''))\n2478 |     elif not isinstance(symbol, Symbol):\n2479 |         f, s, swap = recast_to_symbols([f], [symbol])\n2480 |         # the xreplace will be needed if a ConditionSet is returned\n2481 |         return solveset(f[0], s[0], domain).xreplace(swap)\n2482 | \n2483 |     # solveset should ignore assumptions on symbols\n2484 |     newsym = None\n2485 |     if domain.is_subset(S.Reals):\n2486 |         if symbol._assumptions_orig != {'real': True}:\n2487 |             newsym = Dummy('R', real=True)\n2488 |     elif domain.is_subset(S.Complexes):\n2489 |         if symbol._assumptions_orig != {'complex': True}:\n2490 |             newsym = Dummy('C', complex=True)\n2491 | \n2492 |     if newsym is not None:\n2493 |         rv = solveset(f.xreplace({symbol: newsym}), newsym, domain)\n2494 |         # try to use the original symbol if possible\n2495 |         try:\n2496 |             _rv = rv.xreplace({newsym: symbol})\n2497 |         except TypeError:\n2498 |             _rv = rv\n2499 |         if rv.dummy_eq(_rv):\n2500 |             rv = _rv\n2501 |         return rv\n2502 | \n2503 |     # Abs has its own handling method which avoids the\n2504 |     # rewriting property that the first piece of abs(x)\n2505 |     # is for x >= 0 and the 2nd piece for x < 0 -- solutions\n2506 |     # can look better if the 2nd condition is x <= 0. Since\n2507 |     # the solution is a set, duplication of results is not\n2508 |     # an issue, e.g. {y, -y} when y is 0 will be {0}\n2509 |     f, mask = _masked(f, Abs)\n2510 |     f = f.rewrite(Piecewise) # everything that's not an Abs\n2511 |     for d, e in mask:\n2512 |         # everything *in* an Abs\n2513 |         e = e.func(e.args[0].rewrite(Piecewise))\n2514 |         f = f.xreplace({d: e})\n2515 |     f = piecewise_fold(f)\n2516 | \n2517 |     return _solveset(f, symbol, domain, _check=True)\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/integrals/heurisch.py:heurisch`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n296 | def heurisch(f, x, rewrite=False, hints=None, mappings=None, retries=3,\n297 |              degree_offset=0, unnecessary_permutations=None,\n298 |              _try_heurisch=None):\n299 |     \"\"\"\n300 |     Compute indefinite integral using heuristic Risch algorithm.\n301 | \n302 |     Explanation\n303 |     ===========\n304 | \n305 |     This is a heuristic approach to indefinite integration in finite\n306 |     terms using the extended heuristic (parallel) Risch algorithm, based\n307 |     on Manuel Bronstein's \"Poor Man's Integrator\".\n308 | \n309 |     The algorithm supports various classes of functions including\n310 |     transcendental elementary or special functions like Airy,\n311 |     Bessel, Whittaker and Lambert.\n312 | \n313 |     Note that this algorithm is not a decision procedure. If it isn't\n314 |     able to compute the antiderivative for a given function, then this is\n315 |     not a proof that such a functions does not exist.  One should use\n316 |     recursive Risch algorithm in such case.  It's an open question if\n317 |     this algorithm can be made a full decision procedure.\n318 | \n319 |     This is an internal integrator procedure. You should use top level\n320 |     'integrate' function in most cases, as this procedure needs some\n321 |     preprocessing steps and otherwise may fail.\n322 | \n323 |     Specification\n324 |     =============\n325 | \n326 |      heurisch(f, x, rewrite=False, hints=None)\n327 | \n328 |        where\n329 |          f : expression\n330 |          x : symbol\n331 | \n332 |          rewrite -> force rewrite 'f' in terms of 'tan' and 'tanh'\n333 |          hints   -> a list of functions that may appear in anti-derivate\n334 | \n335 |           - hints = None          --> no suggestions at all\n336 |           - hints = [ ]           --> try to figure out\n337 |           - hints = [f1, ..., fn] --> we know better\n338 | \n339 |     Examples\n340 |     ========\n341 | \n342 |     >>> from sympy import tan\n343 |     >>> from sympy.integrals.heurisch import heurisch\n344 |     >>> from sympy.abc import x, y\n345 | \n346 |     >>> heurisch(y*tan(x), x)\n347 |     y*log(tan(x)**2 + 1)/2\n348 | \n349 |     See Manuel Bronstein's \"Poor Man's Integrator\":\n350 | \n351 |     References\n352 |     ==========\n353 | \n354 |     .. [1] https://www-sop.inria.fr/cafe/Manuel.Bronstein/pmint/index.html\n355 | \n356 |     For more information on the implemented algorithm refer to:\n357 | \n358 |     .. [2] K. Geddes, L. Stefanus, On the Risch-Norman Integration\n359 |        Method and its Implementation in Maple, Proceedings of\n360 |        ISSAC'89, ACM Press, 212-217.\n361 | \n362 |     .. [3] J. H. Davenport, On the Parallel Risch Algorithm (I),\n363 |        Proceedings of EUROCAM'82, LNCS 144, Springer, 144-157.\n364 | \n365 |     .. [4] J. H. Davenport, On the Parallel Risch Algorithm (III):\n366 |        Use of Tangents, SIGSAM Bulletin 16 (1982), 3-6.\n367 | \n368 |     .. [5] J. H. Davenport, B. M. Trager, On the Parallel Risch\n369 |        Algorithm (II), ACM Transactions on Mathematical\n370 |        Software 11 (1985), 356-362.\n371 | \n372 |     See Also\n373 |     ========\n374 | \n375 |     sympy.integrals.integrals.Integral.doit\n376 |     sympy.integrals.integrals.Integral\n377 |     sympy.integrals.heurisch.components\n378 |     \"\"\"\n379 |     f = sympify(f)\n380 | \n381 |     # There are some functions that Heurisch cannot currently handle,\n382 |     # so do not even try.\n383 |     # Set _try_heurisch=True to skip this check\n384 |     if _try_heurisch is not True:\n385 |         if f.has(Abs, re, im, sign, Heaviside, DiracDelta, floor, ceiling, arg):\n386 |             return\n387 | \n388 |     if not f.has_free(x):\n389 |         return f*x\n390 | \n391 |     if not f.is_Add:\n392 |         indep, f = f.as_independent(x)\n393 |     else:\n394 |         indep = S.One\n395 | \n396 |     rewritables = {\n397 |         (sin, cos, cot): tan,\n398 |         (sinh, cosh, coth): tanh,\n399 |     }\n400 | \n401 |     if rewrite:\n402 |         for candidates, rule in rewritables.items():\n403 |             f = f.rewrite(candidates, rule)\n404 |     else:\n405 |         for candidates in rewritables.keys():\n406 |             if f.has(*candidates):\n407 |                 break\n408 |         else:\n409 |             rewrite = True\n410 | \n411 |     terms = components(f, x)\n412 |     dcache = DiffCache(x)\n413 | \n414 |     if hints is not None:\n415 |         if not hints:\n416 |             a = Wild('a', exclude=[x])\n417 |             b = Wild('b', exclude=[x])\n418 |             