tainer(bar_container)\n2628 | \n2629 |         if tick_labels is not None:\n2630 |             tick_labels = np.broadcast_to(tick_labels, len(patches))\n2631 |             tick_label_axis.set_ticks(tick_label_position)\n2632 |             tick_label_axis.set_ticklabels(tick_labels)\n2633 | \n2634 |         return bar_container\n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.barh`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2638 | def barh(self, y, width, height=0.8, left=None, *, align=\"center\",\n2639 |              data=None, **kwargs):\n2640 |         r\"\"\"\n2641 |         Make a horizontal bar plot.\n2642 | \n2643 |         The bars are positioned at *y* with the given *align*\\ment. Their\n2644 |         dimensions are given by *width* and *height*. The horizontal baseline\n2645 |         is *left* (default 0).\n2646 | \n2647 |         Many parameters can take either a single value applying to all bars\n2648 |         or a sequence of values, one for each bar.\n2649 | \n2650 |         Parameters\n2651 |         ----------\n2652 |         y : float or array-like\n2653 |             The y coordinates of the bars. See also *align* for the\n2654 |             alignment of the bars to the coordinates.\n2655 | \n2656 |         width : float or array-like\n2657 |             The width(s) of the bars.\n2658 | \n2659 |             Note that if *left* has units (e.g. datetime), *width* should be in\n2660 |             units that are a difference from the value of *left* (e.g. timedelta).\n2661 | \n2662 |         height : float or array-like, default: 0.8\n2663 |             The heights of the bars.\n2664 | \n2665 |             Note that if *y* has units (e.g. datetime), then *height* should be in\n2666 |             units that are a difference (e.g. timedelta) around the *y* values.\n2667 | \n2668 |         left : float or array-like, default: 0\n2669 |             The x coordinates of the left side(s) of the bars.\n2670 | \n2671 |             Note that if *left* has units, then the x-axis will get a Locator and\n2672 |             Formatter appropriate for the units (e.g. dates, or categorical).\n2673 | \n2674 |         align : {'center', 'edge'}, default: 'center'\n2675 |             Alignment of the base to the *y* coordinates*:\n2676 | \n2677 |             - 'center': Center the bars on the *y* positions.\n2678 |             - 'edge': Align the bottom edges of the bars with the *y*\n2679 |               positions.\n2680 | \n2681 |             To align the bars on the top edge pass a negative *height* and\n2682 |             ``align='edge'``.\n2683 | \n2684 |         Returns\n2685 |         -------\n2686 |         `.BarContainer`\n2687 |             Container with all the bars and optionally errorbars.\n2688 | \n2689 |         Other Parameters\n2690 |         ----------------\n2691 |         color : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2692 |             The colors of the bar faces.\n2693 | \n2694 |         edgecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2695 |             The colors of the bar edges.\n2696 | \n2697 |         linewidth : float or array-like, optional\n2698 |             Width of the bar edge(s). If 0, don't draw edges.\n2699 | \n2700 |         tick_label : str or list of str, optional\n2701 |             The tick labels of the bars.\n2702 |             Default: None (Use default numeric labels.)\n2703 | \n2704 |         label : str or list of str, optional\n2705 |             A single label is attached to the resulting `.BarContainer` as a\n2706 |             label for the whole dataset.\n2707 |             If a list is provided, it must be the same length as *y* and\n2708 |             labels the individual bars. Repeated labels are not de-duplicated\n2709 |             and will cause repeated label entries, so this is best used when\n2710 |             bars also differ in style (e.g., by passing a list to *color*.)\n2711 | \n2712 |         xerr, yerr : float or array-like of shape(N,) or shape(2, N), optional\n2713 |             If not *None*, add horizontal / vertical errorbars to the bar tips.\n2714 |             The values are +/- sizes relative to the data:\n2715 | \n2716 |             - scalar: symmetric +/- values for all bars\n2717 |             - shape(N,): symmetric +/- values for each bar\n2718 |             - shape(2, N): Separate - and + values for each bar. First row\n2719 |               contains the lower errors, the second row contains the upper\n2720 |               errors.\n2721 |             - *None*: No errorbar. (default)\n2722 | \n2723 |             See :doc:`/gallery/statistics/errorbar_features` for an example on\n2724 |             the usage of *xerr* and *yerr*.\n2725 | \n2726 |         ecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, default: 'black'\n2727 |             The line color of the errorbars.\n2728 | \n2729 |         capsize : float, default: :rc:`errorbar.capsize`\n2730 |            The length of the error bar caps in points.\n2731 | \n2732 |         error_kw : dict, optional\n2733 |             Dictionary of keyword arguments to be passed to the\n2734 |             `~.Axes.errorbar` method. Values of *ecolor* or *capsize* defined\n2735 |             here take precedence over the independent keyword arguments.\n2736 | \n2737 |         log : bool, default: False\n2738 |             If ``True``, set the x-axis to be log scale.\n2739 | \n2740 |         data : indexable object, optional\n2741 |             If given, all parameters also accept a string ``s``, which is\n2742 |             interpreted as ``data[s]`` if  ``s`` is a key in ``data``.\n2743 | \n2744 |         **kwargs : `.Rectangle` properties\n2745 | \n2746 |         %(Rectangle:kwdoc)s\n2747 | \n2748 |         See Also\n2749 |         --------\n2750 |         bar : Plot a vertical bar plot.\n2751 | \n2752 |         Notes\n2753 |         -----\n2754 |         Stacked bars can be achieved by passing individual *left* values per\n2755 |         bar. See\n2756 |         :doc:`/gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/horizontal_barchart_distribution`.\n2757 |         \"\"\"\n2758 |         kwargs.setdefault('orientation', 'horizontal')\n2759 |         patches = self.bar(x=left, height=height, width=width, bottom=y,\n2760 |                            align=align, data=data, **kwargs)\n2761 |         return patches\n```\n\nFound function `lib/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/axes3d.py:Axes3D.bar3d`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3049 | def bar3d(self, x, y, z, dx, dy, dz, color=None,\n3050 |               zsort='average', shade=True, lightsource=None, *args,\n3051 |               axlim_clip=False, **kwargs):\n3052 |         \"\"\"\n3053 |         Generate a 3D barplot.\n3054 | \n3055 |         This method creates three-dimensional barplot where the width,\n3056 |         depth, height, and color of the bars can all be uniquely set.\n3057 | \n3058 |         Parameters\n3059 |         ----------\n3060 |         x, y, z : array-like\n3061 |             The coordinates of the anchor point of the bars.\n3062 | \n3063 |         dx, dy, dz : float or array-like\n3064 |             The width, depth, and height of the bars, respectively.\n3065 | \n3066 |         color : sequence of colors, optional\n3067 |             The color of the bars can be specified globally or\n3068 |             individually. This parameter can be:\n3069 | \n3070 |             - A single color, to color all bars the same color.\n3071 |             - An array of colors of length N bars, to color each bar\n3072 |               independently.\n3073 |             - An array of colors of length 6, to color the faces of the\n3074 |               bars similarly.\n3075 |             - An array of colors of length 6 * N bars, to color each face\n3076 |               independently.\n3077 | \n3078 |             When coloring the faces of the boxes specifically, this is\n3079 |             the order of the coloring:\n3080 | \n3081 |             1. -Z (bottom of box)\n3082 |             2. +Z (top of box)\n3083 |             3. -Y\n3084 |             4. +Y\n3085 |             5. -X\n3086 |             6. +X\n3087 | \n3088 |         zsort : {'average', 'min', 'max'}, default: 'average'\n3089 |             The z-axis sorting scheme passed onto `~.art3d.Poly3DCollection`\n3090 | \n3091 |         shade : bool, default: True\n3092 |             When true, this shades the dark sides of the bars (relative\n3093 |             to the plot's source of light).\n3094 | \n3095 |         lightsource : `~matplotlib.colors.LightSource`, optional\n3096 |             The lightsource to use when *shade* is True.\n3097 | \n3098 |         axlim_clip : bool, default: False\n3099 |             Whether to hide the bars with points outside the axes view limits.\n3100 | \n3101 |             .. versionadded:: 3.10\n3102 | \n3103 |         data : indexable object, optional\n3104 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n3105 | \n3106 |         **kwargs\n3107 |             Any additional keyword arguments are passed onto\n3108 |             `~.art3d.Poly3DCollection`.\n3109 | \n3110 |         Returns\n3111 |         -------\n3112 |         collection : `~.art3d.Poly3DCollection`\n3113 |             A collection of three-dimensional polygons representing the bars.\n3114 |         \"\"\"\n3115 | \n3116 |         had_data = self.has_data()\n3117 | \n3118 |         x, y, z, dx, dy, dz = np.broadcast_arrays(\n3119 |             np.atleast_1d(x), y, z, dx, dy, dz)\n3120 |         minx = np.min(x)\n3121 |         maxx = np.max(x + dx)\n3122 |         miny = np.min(y)\n3123 |         maxy = np.max(y + dy)\n3124 |         minz = np.min(z)\n3125 |         maxz = np.max(z + dz)\n3126 | \n3127 |         # shape (6, 4, 3)\n3128 |         # All faces are oriented facing outwards - when viewed from the\n3129 |         # outside, their vertices are in a counterclockwise ordering.\n3130 |         cuboid = np.array([\n3131 |             # -z\n3132 |             (\n3133 |                 (0, 0, 0),\n3134 |                 (0, 1, 0),\n3135 |                 (1, 1, 0),\n3136 |                 (1, 0, 0),\n3137 |             ),\n3138 |             # +z\n3139 |             (\n3140 |                 (0, 0, 1),\n3141 |                 (1, 0, 1),\n3142 |                 (1, 1, 1),\n3143 |                 (0, 1, 1),\n3144 |             ),\n3145 |             # -y\n3146 |             (\n3147 |                 (0, 0, 0),\n3148 |                 (1, 0, 0),\n3149 |                 (1, 0, 1),\n3150 |                 (0, 0, 1),\n3151 |             ),\n3152 |             # +y\n3153 |             (\n3154 |                 (0, 1, 0),\n3155 |                 (0, 1, 1),\n3156 |                 (1, 1, 1),\n3157 |                 (1, 1, 0),\n3158 |             ),\n3159 |             # -x\n3160 |             (\n3161 |                 (0, 0, 0),\n3162 |                 (0, 0, 1),\n3163 |                 (0, 1, 1),\n3164 |                 (0, 1, 0),\n3165 |             ),\n3166 |             # +x\n3167 |             (\n3168 |                 (1, 0, 0),\n3169 |                 (1, 1, 0),\n3170 |                 (1, 1, 1),\n3171 |                 (1, 0, 1),\n3172 |             ),\n3173 |         ])\n3174 | \n3175 |         # indexed by [bar, face, vertex, coord]\n3176 |         polys = np.empty(x.shape + cuboid.shape)\n3177 | \n3178 |         # handle each coordinate separately\n3179 |         for i, p, dp in [(0, x, dx), (1, y, dy), (2, z, dz)]:\n3180 |             p = p[..., np.newaxis, np.newaxis]\n3181 |             dp = dp[..., np.newaxis, np.newaxis]\n3182 |             polys[..., i] = p + dp * cuboid[..., i]\n3183 | \n3184 |         # collapse the first two axes\n3185 |         polys = polys.reshape((-1,) + polys.shape[2:])\n3186 | \n3187 |         facecolors = []\n3188 |         if color is None:\n3189 |             color = [self._get_patches_for_fill.get_next_color()]\n3190 | \n3191 |         color = list(mcolors.to_rgba_array(color))\n3192 | \n3193 |         if len(color) == len(x):\n3194 |             # bar colors specified, need to expand to number of faces\n3195 |             for c in color:\n3196 |                 facecolors.extend([c] * 6)\n3197 |         else:\n3198 |             # a single color specified, or face colors specified explicitly\n3199 |             facecolors = color\n3200 |             if len(facecolors) < len(x):\n3201 |                 facecolors *= (6 * len(x))\n3202 | \n3203 |         col = art3d.Poly3DCollection(polys,\n3204 |                                      zsort=zsort,\n3205 |                                      facecolors=facecolors,\n3206 |                                      shade=shade,\n3207 |                                      lightsource=lightsource,\n3208 |                                      axlim_clip=axlim_clip,\n3209 |                                      *args, **kwargs)\n3210 |         self.add_collection(col)\n3211 | \n3212 |         self.auto_scale_xyz((minx, maxx), (miny, maxy), (minz, maxz), had_data)\n3213 | \n3214 |         return col\n```\n\nFound function `lib/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/axes3d.py:Axes3D.bar`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2989 | def bar(self, left, height, zs=0, zdir='z', *args,\n2990 |             axlim_clip=False, **kwargs):\n2991 |         \"\"\"\n2992 |         Add 2D bar(s).\n2993 | \n2994 |         Parameters\n2995 |         ----------\n2996 |         left : 1D array-like\n2997 |             The x coordinates of the left sides of the bars.\n2998 |         height : 1D array-like\n2999 |             The height of the bars.\n3000 |         zs : float or 1D array-like, default: 0\n3001 |             Z coordinate of bars; if a single value is specified, it will be\n3002 |             used for all bars.\n3003 |         zdir : {'x', 'y', 'z'}, default: 'z'\n3004 |             When plotting 2D data, the direction to use as z ('x', 'y' or 'z').\n3005 |         axlim_clip : bool, default: False\n3006 |             Whether to hide bars with points outside the axes view limits.\n3007 | \n3008 |             .. versionadded:: 3.10\n3009 |         data : indexable object, optional\n3010 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n3011 |         **kwargs\n3012 |             Other keyword arguments are forwarded to\n3013 |             `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar`.\n3014 | \n3015 |         Returns\n3016 |         -------\n3017 |         mpl_toolkits.mplot3d.art3d.Patch3DCollection\n3018 |         \"\"\"\n3019 |         had_data = self.has_data()\n3020 | \n3021 |         patches = super().bar(left, height, *args, **kwargs)\n3022 | \n3023 |         zs = np.broadcast_to(zs, len(left), subok=True)\n3024 | \n3025 |         verts = []\n3026 |         verts_zs = []\n3027 |         for p, z in zip(patches, zs):\n3028 |             vs = art3d._get_patch_verts(p)\n3029 |             verts += vs.tolist()\n3030 |             verts_zs += [z] * len(vs)\n3031 |             art3d.patch_2d_to_3d(p, z, zdir, axlim_clip)\n3032 |             if 'alpha' in kwargs:\n3033 |                 p.set_alpha(kwargs['alpha'])\n3034 | \n3035 |         if len(verts) > 0:\n3036 |             # the following has to be skipped if verts is empty\n3037 |             # NOTE: Bugs could still occur if len(verts) > 0,\n3038 |             #       but the \"2nd dimension\" is empty.\n3039 |             xs, ys = zip(*verts)\n3040 |         else:\n3041 |             xs, ys = [], []\n3042 | \n3043 |         xs, ys, verts_zs = art3d.juggle_axes(xs, ys, verts_zs, zdir)\n3044 |         self.auto_scale_xyz(xs, ys, verts_zs, had_data)\n3045 | \n3046 |         return patches\n```\n\nFound class `lib/matplotlib/container.py:BarContainer`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n42 | class BarContainer(Container):\n43 |     \"\"\"\n44 |     Container for the artists of bar plots (e.g. created by `.Axes.bar`).\n45 | \n46 |     The container can be treated as a tuple of the *patches* themselves.\n47 |     Additionally, you can access these and further parameters by the\n48 |     attributes.\n49 | \n50 |     Attributes\n51 |     ----------\n52 |     patches : list of :class:`~matplotlib.patches.Rectangle`\n53 |         The artists of the bars.\n54 | \n55 |     errorbar : None or :class:`~matplotlib.container.ErrorbarContainer`\n56 |         A container for the error bar artists if error bars are present.\n57 |         *None* otherwise.\n58 | \n59 |     datavalues : None or array-like\n60 |         The underlying data values corresponding to the bars.\n61 | \n62 |     orientation : {'vertical', 'horizontal'}, default: None\n63 |         If 'vertical', the bars are assumed to be vertical.\n64 |         If 'horizontal', the bars are assumed to be horizontal.\n65 | \n66 |     \"\"\"\n67 | \n68 |     def __init__(self, patches, errorbar=None, *, datavalues=None,\n69 |                  orientation=None, **kwargs):\n70 |         self.patches = patches\n71 |         self.errorbar = errorbar\n72 |         self.datavalues = datavalues\n73 |         self.orientation = orientation\n74 |         super().__init__(patches, **kwargs)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"scatter()\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/shapes_and_collections/scatter.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ============\n 3 | Scatter plot\n 4 | ============\n 5 | \n 6 | This example showcases a simple scatter plot.\n 7 | \"\"\"\n 8 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 9 | import numpy as np\n10 | \n11 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n12 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n13 | \n14 | \n15 | N = 50\n16 | x = np.random.rand(N)\n17 | y = np.random.rand(N)\n18 | colors = np.random.rand(N)\n19 | area = (30 * np.random.rand(N))**2  # 0 to 15 point radii\n20 | \n21 | plt.scatter(x, y, s=area, c=colors, alpha=0.5)\n22 | plt.show()\n23 | \n24 | # %%\n25 | #\n26 | # .. admonition:: References\n27 | #\n28 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n29 | #    in this example:\n30 | #\n31 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n32 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/lines_bars_and_markers/scatter_with_legend.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==========================\n  3 | Scatter plot with a legend\n  4 | ==========================\n  5 | \n  6 | To create a scatter plot with a legend one may use a loop and create one\n  7 | `~.Axes.scatter` plot per item to appear in the legend and set the ``label``\n  8 | accordingly.\n  9 | \n 10 | The following also demonstrates how transparency of the markers\n 11 | can be adjusted by giving ``alpha`` a value between 0 and 1.\n 12 | \"\"\"\n 13 | \n 14 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 15 | import numpy as np\n 16 | \n 17 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n 18 | \n 19 | \n 20 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 21 | for color in ['tab:blue', 'tab:orange', 'tab:green']:\n 22 |     n = 750\n 23 |     x, y = np.random.rand(2, n)\n 24 |     scale = 200.0 * np.random.rand(n)\n 25 |     ax.scatter(x, y, c=color, s=scale, label=color,\n 26 |                alpha=0.3, edgecolors='none')\n 27 | \n 28 | ax.legend()\n 29 | ax.grid(True)\n 30 | \n 31 | plt.show()\n 32 | \n 33 | \n 34 | # %%\n 35 | # .. _automatedlegendcreation:\n 36 | #\n 37 | # Automated legend creation\n 38 | # -------------------------\n 39 | #\n 40 | # Another option for creating a legend for a scatter is to use the\n 41 | # `.PathCollection.legend_elements` method.  It will automatically try to\n 42 | # determine a useful number of legend entries to be shown and return a tuple of\n 43 | # handles and labels. Those can be passed to the call to `~.axes.Axes.legend`.\n 44 | \n 45 | \n 46 | N = 45\n 47 | x, y = np.random.rand(2, N)\n 48 | c = np.random.randint(1, 5, size=N)\n 49 | s = np.random.randint(10, 220, size=N)\n 50 | \n 51 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 52 | \n 53 | scatter = ax.scatter(x, y, c=c, s=s)\n 54 | \n 55 | # produce a legend with the unique colors from the scatter\n 56 | legend1 = ax.legend(*scatter.legend_elements(),\n 57 |                     loc=\"lower left\", title=\"Classes\")\n 58 | ax.add_artist(legend1)\n 59 | \n 60 | # produce a legend with a cross-section of sizes from the scatter\n 61 | handles, labels = scatter.legend_elements(prop=\"sizes\", alpha=0.6)\n 62 | legend2 = ax.legend(handles, labels, loc=\"upper right\", title=\"Sizes\")\n 63 | \n 64 | plt.show()\n 65 | \n 66 | \n 67 | # %%\n 68 | # Further arguments to the `.PathCollection.legend_elements` method\n 69 | # can be used to steer how many legend entries are to be created and how they\n 70 | # should be labeled. The following shows how to use some of them.\n 71 | \n 72 | volume = np.random.rayleigh(27, size=40)\n 73 | amount = np.random.poisson(10, size=40)\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 1 | \"\"\"\n 2 | ==========================\n 3 | Scatter plot on polar axis\n 4 | ==========================\n 5 | \n 6 | Size increases radially in this example and color increases with angle\n 7 | (just to verify the symbols are being scattered correctly).\n 8 | \"\"\"\n 9 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n10 | import numpy as np\n11 | \n12 | # Fixing random state for reproducibility\n13 | np.random.seed(19680801)\n14 | \n15 | # Compute areas and colors\n16 | N = 150\n17 | r = 2 * np.random.rand(N)\n18 | theta = 2 * np.pi * np.random.rand(N)\n19 | area = 200 * r**2\n20 | colors = theta\n21 | \n22 | fig = plt.figure()\n23 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n24 | c = ax.scatter(theta, r, c=colors, s=area, cmap='hsv', alpha=0.75)\n25 | \n26 | # %%\n27 | # Scatter plot on polar axis, with offset origin\n28 | # ----------------------------------------------\n29 | #\n30 | # The main difference with the previous plot is the configuration of the origin\n31 | # radius, producing an annulus. Additionally, the theta zero location is set to\n32 | # rotate the plot.\n33 | \n34 | fig = plt.figure()\n35 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n36 | c = ax.scatter(theta, r, c=colors, s=area, cmap='hsv', alpha=0.75)\n37 | \n38 | ax.set_rorigin(-2.5)\n39 | ax.set_theta_zero_location('W', offset=10)\n40 | \n41 | # %%\n42 | # Scatter plot on polar axis confined to a sector\n43 | # -----------------------------------------------\n44 | #\n45 | # The main difference with the previous plots is the configuration of the\n46 | # theta start and end limits, producing a sector instead of a full circle.\n47 | \n48 | fig = plt.figure()\n49 | ax = fig.add_subplot(projection='polar')\n50 | c = ax.scatter(theta, r, c=colors, s=area, cmap='hsv', alpha=0.75)\n51 | \n52 | ax.set_thetamin(45)\n53 | ax.set_thetamax(135)\n54 | \n55 | plt.show()\n56 | \n57 | # %%\n58 | #\n59 | # .. admonition:: References\n60 | #\n61 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n62 | #    in this example:\n63 | #\n64 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n65 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar`\n66 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_rorigin`\n67 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_theta_zero_location`\n68 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_thetamin`\n69 | #    - `matplotlib.projections.polar.PolarAxes.set_thetamax`\n70 | #\n71 | # .. tags::\n72 | #\n73 | #    plot-style: polar\n74 | #    plot-style: scatter\n75 | #    level: beginner\n76 | \n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 74 | ranking = np.random.normal(size=40)\n 75 | price = np.random.uniform(1, 10, size=40)\n 76 | \n 77 | fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n 78 | \n 79 | # Because the price is much too small when being provided as size for ``s``,\n 80 | # we normalize it to some useful point sizes, s=0.3*(price*3)**2\n 81 | scatter = ax.scatter(volume, amount, c=ranking, s=0.3*(price*3)**2,\n 82 |                      vmin=-3, vmax=3, cmap=\"Spectral\")\n 83 | \n 84 | # Produce a legend for the ranking (colors). Even though there are 40 different\n 85 | # rankings, we only want to show 5 of them in the legend.\n 86 | legend1 = ax.legend(*scatter.legend_elements(num=5),\n 87 |                     loc=\"upper left\", title=\"Ranking\")\n 88 | ax.add_artist(legend1)\n 89 | \n 90 | # Produce a legend for the price (sizes). Because we want to show the prices\n 91 | # in dollars, we use the *func* argument to supply the inverse of the function\n 92 | # used to calculate the sizes from above. The *fmt* ensures to show the price\n 93 | # in dollars. Note how we target at 5 elements here, but obtain only 4 in the\n 94 | # created legend due to the automatic round prices that are chosen for us.\n 95 | kw = dict(prop=\"sizes\", num=5, color=scatter.cmap(0.7), fmt=\"$ {x:.2f}\",\n 96 |           func=lambda s: np.sqrt(s/.3)/3)\n 97 | legend2 = ax.legend(*scatter.legend_elements(**kw),\n 98 |                     loc=\"lower right\", title=\"Price\")\n 99 | \n100 | plt.show()\n101 | \n102 | # %%\n103 | #\n104 | # .. admonition:: References\n105 | #\n106 | #    The use of the following functions, methods, classes and modules is shown\n107 | #    in this example:\n108 | #\n109 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.scatter` / `matplotlib.pyplot.scatter`\n110 | #    - `matplotlib.axes.Axes.legend` / `matplotlib.pyplot.legend`\n111 | #    - `matplotlib.collections.PathCollection.legend_elements`\n112 | #\n113 | # .. tags::\n114 | #\n115 | #    component: legend\n116 | #    plot-type: scatter\n117 | #    level: intermediate\n118 | \n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.scatter`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n4694 | def scatter(self, x, y, s=None, c=None, marker=None, cmap=None, norm=None,\n4695 |                 vmin=None, vmax=None, alpha=None, linewidths=None, *,\n4696 |                 edgecolors=None, colorizer=None, plotnonfinite=False, **kwargs):\n4697 |         \"\"\"\n4698 |         A scatter plot of *y* vs. *x* with varying marker size and/or color.\n4699 | \n4700 |         Parameters\n4701 |         ----------\n4702 |         x, y : float or array-like, shape (n, )\n4703 |             The data positions.\n4704 | \n4705 |         s : float or array-like, shape (n, ), optional\n4706 |             The marker size in points**2 (typographic points are 1/72 in.).\n4707 |             Default is ``rcParams['lines.markersize'] ** 2``.\n4708 | \n4709 |             The linewidth and edgecolor can visually interact with the marker\n4710 |             size, and can lead to artifacts if the marker size is smaller than\n4711 |             the linewidth.\n4712 | \n4713 |             If the linewidth is greater than 0 and the edgecolor is anything\n4714 |             but *'none'*, then the effective size of the marker will be\n4715 |             increased by half the linewidth because the stroke will be centered\n4716 |             on the edge of the shape.\n4717 | \n4718 |             To eliminate the marker edge either set *linewidth=0* or\n4719 |             *edgecolor='none'*.\n4720 | \n4721 |         c : array-like or list of :mpltype:`color` or :mpltype:`color`, optional\n4722 |             The marker colors. Possible values:\n4723 | \n4724 |             - A scalar or sequence of n numbers to be mapped to colors using\n4725 |               *cmap* and *norm*.\n4726 |             - A 2D array in which the rows are RGB or RGBA.\n4727 |             - A sequence of colors of length n.\n4728 |             - A single color format string.\n4729 | \n4730 |             Note that *c* should not be a single numeric RGB or RGBA sequence\n4731 |             because that is indistinguishable from an array of values to be\n4732 |             colormapped. If you want to specify the same RGB or RGBA value for\n4733 |             all points,