   `scipy.interpolate.interp1d`, whereas 'spline' is passed to\n7676 |               `scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline`. These methods use the numerical\n7677 |               values of the index.  Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that\n7678 |               you also specify an `order` (int), e.g.\n7679 |               ``df.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5)``. Note that,\n7680 |               `slinear` method in Pandas refers to the Scipy first order `spline`\n7681 |               instead of Pandas first order `spline`.\n7682 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n7683 |               'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods of\n7684 |               similar names. See `Notes`.\n7685 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to\n7686 |               `scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives`.\n7687 | \n7688 |         axis : {{0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}}, default None\n7689 |             Axis to interpolate along. For `Series` this parameter is unused\n7690 |             and defaults to 0.\n7691 |         limit : int, optional\n7692 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than\n7693 |             0.\n7694 |         inplace : bool, default False\n7695 |             Update the data in place if possible.\n7696 |         limit_direction : {{'forward', 'backward', 'both'}}, optional, default 'forward'\n7697 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n7698 | \n7699 |         limit_area : {{`None`, 'inside', 'outside'}}, default None\n7700 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n7701 |             restriction.\n7702 | \n7703 |             * ``None``: No fill restriction.\n7704 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values\n7705 |               (interpolate).\n7706 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n7707 | \n7708 |         **kwargs : optional\n7709 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n7710 | \n7711 |         Returns\n7712 |         -------\n7713 |         Series or DataFrame or None\n7714 |             Returns the same object type as the caller, interpolated at\n7715 |             some or all ``NaN`` values or None if ``inplace=True``.\n7716 | \n7717 |         See Also\n7718 |         --------\n7719 |         fillna : Fill missing values using different methods.\n7720 |         scipy.interpolate.Akima1DInterpolator : Piecewise cubic polynomials\n7721 |             (Akima interpolator).\n7722 |         scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives : Piecewise polynomial in the\n7723 |             Bernstein basis.\n7724 |         scipy.interpolate.interp1d : Interpolate a 1-D function.\n7725 |         scipy.interpolate.KroghInterpolator : Interpolate polynomial (Krogh\n7726 |             interpolator).\n7727 |         scipy.interpolate.PchipInterpolator : PCHIP 1-d monotonic cubic\n7728 |             interpolation.\n7729 |         scipy.interpolate.CubicSpline : Cubic spline data interpolator.\n7730 | \n7731 |         Notes\n7732 |         -----\n7733 |         The 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip' and 'akima'\n7734 |         methods are wrappers around the respective SciPy implementations of\n7735 |         similar names. These use the actual numerical values of the index.\n7736 |         For more information on their behavior, see the\n7737 |         `SciPy documentation\n7738 |         <https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/interpolate.html#univariate-interpolation>`__.\n7739 | \n7740 |         Examples\n7741 |         --------\n7742 |         Filling in ``NaN`` in a :class:`~pandas.Series` via linear\n7743 |         interpolation.\n7744 | \n7745 |         >>> s = pd.Series([0, 1, np.nan, 3])\n7746 |         >>> s\n7747 |         0    0.0\n7748 |         1    1.0\n7749 |         2    NaN\n7750 |         3    3.0\n7751 |         dtype: float64\n7752 |         >>> s.interpolate()\n7753 |         0    0.0\n7754 |         1    1.0\n7755 |         2    2.0\n7756 |         3    3.0\n7757 |         dtype: float64\n7758 | \n7759 |         Filling in ``NaN`` in a Series via polynomial interpolation or splines:\n7760 |         Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' methods require that you also specify\n7761 |         an ``order`` (int).\n7762 | \n7763 |         >>> s = pd.Series([0, 2, np.nan, 8])\n7764 |         >>> s.interpolate(method=\"polynomial\", order=2)\n7765 |         0    0.000000\n7766 |         1    2.000000\n7767 |         2    4.666667\n7768 |         3    8.000000\n7769 |         dtype: float64\n7770 | \n7771 |         Fill the DataFrame forward (that is, going down) along each column\n7772 |         using linear interpolation.\n7773 | \n7774 |         Note how the last entry in column 'a' is interpolated differently,\n7775 |         because there is no entry after it to use for interpolation.\n7776 |         Note how the first entry in column 'b' remains ``NaN``, because there\n7777 |         is no entry before it to use for interpolation.\n7778 | \n7779 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n7780 |         ...     [\n7781 |         ...         (0.0, np.nan, -1.0, 1.0),\n7782 |         ...         (np.nan, 2.0, np.nan, np.nan),\n7783 |         ...         (2.0, 3.0, np.nan, 9.0),\n7784 |         ...         (np.nan, 4.0, -4.0, 16.0),\n7785 |         ...     ],\n7786 |         ...     columns=list(\"abcd\"),\n7787 |         ... )\n7788 |         >>> df\n7789 |              a    b    c     d\n7790 |         0  0.0  NaN -1.0   1.0\n7791 |         1  NaN  2.0  NaN   NaN\n7792 |         2  2.0  3.0  NaN   9.0\n7793 |         3  NaN  4.0 -4.0  16.0\n7794 |         >>> df.interpolate(method=\"linear\", limit_direction=\"forward\", axis=0)\n7795 |              a    b    c     d\n7796 |         0  0.0  NaN -1.0   1.0\n7797 |         1  1.0  2.0 -2.0   5.0\n7798 |         2  2.0  3.0 -3.0   9.0\n7799 |         3  2.0  4.0 -4.0  16.0\n7800 | \n7801 |         Using polynomial interpolation.\n7802 | \n7803 |         >>> df[\"d\"].interpolate(method=\"polynomial\", order=2)\n7804 |         0     1.0\n7805 |         1     4.0\n7806 |         2     9.0\n7807 |         3    16.0\n7808 |         Name: d, dtype: float64\n7809 |         \"\"\"\n7810 |         inplace = validate_bool_kwarg(inplace, \"inplace\")\n7811 | \n7812 |         if inplace:\n7813 |             if not PYPY:\n7814 |                 if sys.getrefcount(self) <= REF_COUNT:\n7815 |                     warnings.warn(\n7816 |                         _chained_assignment_method_msg,\n7817 |                         ChainedAssignmentError,\n7818 |                         stacklevel=2,\n7819 |                     )\n7820 | \n7821 |         axis = self._get_axis_number(axis)\n7822 | \n7823 |         if self.empty:\n7824 |             if inplace:\n7825 |                 return None\n7826 |             return self.copy()\n7827 | \n7828 |         if not isinstance(method, str):\n7829 |             raise ValueError(\"'method' should be a string, not None.\")\n7830 | \n7831 |         obj, should_transpose = (self.T, True) if axis == 1 else (self, False)\n7832 | \n7833 |         if isinstance(obj.index, MultiIndex) and method != \"linear\":\n7834 |             raise ValueError(\n7835 |                 \"Only `method=linear` interpolation is supported on MultiIndexes.\"\n7836 |             )\n7837 | \n7838 |         limit_direction = missing.infer_limit_direction(limit_direction, method)\n7839 | \n7840 |         index = missing.get_interp_index(method, obj.index)\n7841 |         new_data = obj._mgr.interpolate(\n7842 |             method=method,\n7843 |             index=index,\n7844 |             limit=limit,\n7845 |             limit_direction=limit_direction,\n7846 |             limit_area=limit_area,\n7847 |             inplace=inplace,\n7848 |             **kwargs,\n7849 |         )\n7850 | \n7851 |         result = self._constructor_from_mgr(new_data, axes=new_data.axes)\n7852 |         if should_transpose:\n7853 |             result = result.T\n7854 |         if inplace:\n7855 |             return self._update_inplace(result)\n7856 |         else:\n7857 |             return result.__finalize__(self, method=\"interpolate\")\n```\n"}, {"content": "After analyzing the code and issue, here are the relevant locations that need documentation improvements:\n\n```\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 7658-7836\nfunction: NDFrame.interpolate\n\npandas/core/arrays/base.py\nline: 989-1097\nfunction: ExtensionArray.interpolate\n\npandas/core/resample.py\nline: 585-604\nclass: Interpolate\n\npandas/core/internals/blocks.py\nline: 1160-1250\nfunction: Block.interpolate\n\npandas/core/internals/managers.py\nline: 1310-1410\nfunction: BaseBlockManager.interpolate\n```\n\nThese locations were selected based on:\n\n1. The primary DataFrame.interpolate() method implementation in pandas/core/generic.py is the main focus, with key documentation sections needing improvement.\n\n2. Related interpolate implementations in:\n   - ExtensionArray.interpolate (generic array interface)\n   - Interpolate benchmark class (testing infrastructure)\n   - Block.interpolate (low-level data processing)\n   - BaseBlockManager.interpolate (core interpolation logic)\n\n3. The documentation needs clarification in several key areas:\n   - Method parameters and behavior, particularly for 'time', 'index', and 'values' methods\n   - Supported interpolation techniques and their parameters\n   - Special handling for MultiIndex cases\n   - Connection between interpolation methods and time-based indexing\n\n4. These locations together form the complete chain of the interpolation implementation hierarchy, providing both high-level and low-level documentation points.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 93698, "completion_tokens": 616}, "time": 114.63379669189453}]}}
