sv writer write_csv float\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.to_json`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2318 | def to_json(\n2319 |         self,\n2320 |         path_or_buf: FilePath | WriteBuffer[bytes] | WriteBuffer[str] | None = None,\n2321 |         *,\n2322 |         orient: Literal[\"split\", \"records\", \"index\", \"table\", \"columns\", \"values\"]\n2323 |         | None = None,\n2324 |         date_format: str | None = None,\n2325 |         double_precision: int = 10,\n2326 |         force_ascii: bool = True,\n2327 |         date_unit: TimeUnit = \"ms\",\n2328 |         default_handler: Callable[[Any], JSONSerializable] | None = None,\n2329 |         lines: bool = False,\n2330 |         compression: CompressionOptions = \"infer\",\n2331 |         index: bool | None = None,\n2332 |         indent: int | None = None,\n2333 |         storage_options: StorageOptions | None = None,\n2334 |         mode: Literal[\"a\", \"w\"] = \"w\",\n2335 |     ) -> str | None:\n2336 |         \"\"\"\n2337 |         Convert the object to a JSON string.\n2338 | \n2339 |         Note NaN's and None will be converted to null and datetime objects\n2340 |         will be converted to UNIX timestamps.\n2341 | \n2342 |         Parameters\n2343 |         ----------\n2344 |         path_or_buf : str, path object, file-like object, or None, default None\n2345 |             String, path object (implementing os.PathLike[str]), or file-like\n2346 |             object implementing a write() function. If None, the result is\n2347 |             returned as a string.\n2348 |         orient : str\n2349 |             Indication of expected JSON string format.\n2350 | \n2351 |             * Series:\n2352 | \n2353 |                 - default is 'index'\n2354 |                 - allowed values are: {{'split', 'records', 'index', 'table'}}.\n2355 | \n2356 |             * DataFrame:\n2357 | \n2358 |                 - default is 'columns'\n2359 |                 - allowed values are: {{'split', 'records', 'index', 'columns',\n2360 |                   'values', 'table'}}.\n2361 | \n2362 |             * The format of the JSON string:\n2363 | \n2364 |                 - 'split' : dict like {{'index' -> [index], 'columns' -> [columns],\n2365 |                   'data' -> [values]}}\n2366 |                 - 'records' : list like [{{column -> value}}, ... , {{column -> value}}]\n2367 |                 - 'index' : dict like {{index -> {{column -> value}}}}\n2368 |                 - 'columns' : dict like {{column -> {{index -> value}}}}\n2369 |                 - 'values' : just the values array\n2370 |                 - 'table' : dict like {{'schema': {{schema}}, 'data': {{data}}}}\n2371 | \n2372 |                 Describing the data, where data component is like ``orient='records'``.\n2373 | \n2374 |         date_format : {{None, 'epoch', 'iso'}}\n2375 |             Type of date conversion. 'epoch' = epoch milliseconds,\n2376 |             'iso' = ISO8601. The default depends on the `orient`. For\n2377 |             ``orient='table'``, the default is 'iso'. For all other orients,\n2378 |             the default is 'epoch'.\n2379 | \n2380 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n2381 |                 'epoch' date format is deprecated and will be removed in a future\n2382 |                 version, please use 'iso' instead.\n2383 | \n2384 |         double_precision : int, default 10\n2385 |             The number of decimal places to use when encoding\n2386 |             floating point values. The possible maximal value is 15.\n2387 |             Passing double_precision greater than 15 will raise a ValueError.\n2388 |         force_ascii : bool, default True\n2389 |             Force encoded string to be ASCII.\n2390 |         date_unit : str, default 'ms' (milliseconds)\n2391 |             The time unit to encode to, governs timestamp and ISO8601\n2392 |             precision.  One of 's', 'ms', 'us', 'ns' for second, millisecond,\n2393 |             microsecond, and nanosecond respectively.\n2394 |         default_handler : callable, default None\n2395 |             Handler to call if object cannot otherwise be converted to a\n2396 |             suitable format for JSON. Should receive a single argument which is\n2397 |             the object to convert and return a serialisable object.\n2398 |         lines : bool, default False\n2399 |             If 'orient' is 'records' write out line-delimited json format. Will\n2400 |             throw ValueError if incorrect 'orient' since others are not\n2401 |             list-like.\n2402 |         {compression_options}\n2403 | \n2404 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0 Zstandard support.\n2405 | \n2406 |         index : bool or None, default None\n2407 |             The index is only used when 'orient' is 'split', 'index', 'column',\n2408 |             or 'table'. Of these, 'index' and 'column' do not support\n2409 |             `index=False`. The string 'index' as a column name with empty :class:`Index`\n2410 |             or if it is 'index' will raise a ``ValueError``.\n2411 | \n2412 |         indent : int, optional\n2413 |            Length of whitespace used to indent each record.\n2414 | \n2415 |         {storage_options}\n2416 | \n2417 |         mode : str, default 'w' (writing)\n2418 |             Specify the IO mode for output when supplying a path_or_buf.\n2419 |             Accepted args are 'w' (writing) and 'a' (append) only.\n2420 |             mode='a' is only supported when lines is True and orient is 'records'.\n2421 | \n2422 |         Returns\n2423 |         -------\n2424 |         None or str\n2425 |             If path_or_buf is None, returns the resulting json format as a\n2426 |             string. Otherwise returns None.\n2427 | \n2428 |         See Also\n2429 |         --------\n2430 |         read_json : Convert a JSON string to pandas object.\n2431 | \n2432 |         Notes\n2433 |         -----\n2434 |         The behavior of ``indent=0`` varies from the stdlib, which does not\n2435 |         indent the output but does insert newlines. Currently, ``indent=0``\n2436 |         and the default ``indent=None`` are equivalent in pandas, though this\n2437 |         may change in a future release.\n2438 | \n2439 |         ``orient='table'`` contains a 'pandas_version' field under 'schema'.\n2440 |         This stores the version of `pandas` used in the latest revision of the\n2441 |         schema.\n2442 | \n2443 |         Examples\n2444 |         --------\n2445 |         >>> from json import loads, dumps\n2446 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n2447 |         ...     [[\"a\", \"b\"], [\"c\", \"d\"]],\n2448 |         ...     index=[\"row 1\", \"row 2\"],\n2449 |         ...     columns=[\"col 1\", \"col 2\"],\n2450 |         ... )\n2451 | \n2452 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"split\")\n2453 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2454 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2455 |         {{\n2456 |             \"columns\": [\n2457 |                 \"col 1\",\n2458 |                 \"col 2\"\n2459 |             ],\n2460 |             \"index\": [\n2461 |                 \"row 1\",\n2462 |                 \"row 2\"\n2463 |             ],\n2464 |             \"data\": [\n2465 |                 [\n2466 |                     \"a\",\n2467 |                     \"b\"\n2468 |                 ],\n2469 |                 [\n2470 |                     \"c\",\n2471 |                     \"d\"\n2472 |                 ]\n2473 |             ]\n2474 |         }}\n2475 | \n2476 |         Encoding/decoding a Dataframe using ``'records'`` formatted JSON.\n2477 |         Note that index labels are not preserved with this encoding.\n2478 | \n2479 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"records\")\n2480 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2481 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2482 |         [\n2483 |             {{\n2484 |                 \"col 1\": \"a\",\n2485 |                 \"col 2\": \"b\"\n2486 |             }},\n2487 |             {{\n2488 |                 \"col 1\": \"c\",\n2489 |                 \"col 2\": \"d\"\n2490 |             }}\n2491 |         ]\n2492 | \n2493 |         Encoding/decoding a Dataframe using ``'index'`` formatted JSON:\n2494 | \n2495 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"index\")\n2496 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2497 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2498 |         {{\n2499 |             \"row 1\": {{\n2500 |                 \"col 1\": \"a\",\n2501 |                 \"col 2\": \"b\"\n2502 |             }},\n2503 |             \"row 2\": {{\n2504 |                 \"col 1\": \"c\",\n2505 |                 \"col 2\": \"d\"\n2506 |             }}\n2507 |         }}\n2508 | \n2509 |         Encoding/decoding a Dataframe using ``'columns'`` formatted JSON:\n2510 | \n2511 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"columns\")\n2512 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2513 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2514 |         {{\n2515 |             \"col 1\": {{\n2516 |                 \"row 1\": \"a\",\n2517 |                 \"row 2\": \"c\"\n2518 |             }},\n2519 |             \"col 2\": {{\n2520 |                 \"row 1\": \"b\",\n2521 |                 \"row 2\": \"d\"\n2522 |             }}\n2523 |         }}\n2524 | \n2525 |         Encoding/decoding a Dataframe using ``'values'`` formatted JSON:\n2526 | \n2527 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"values\")\n2528 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2529 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2530 |         [\n2531 |             [\n2532 |                 \"a\",\n2533 |                 \"b\"\n2534 |             ],\n2535 |             [\n2536 |                 \"c\",\n2537 |                 \"d\"\n2538 |             ]\n2539 |         ]\n2540 | \n2541 |         Encoding with Table Schema:\n2542 | \n2543 |         >>> result = df.to_json(orient=\"table\")\n2544 |         >>> parsed = loads(result)\n2545 |         >>> dumps(parsed, indent=4)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2546 |         {{\n2547 |             \"schema\": {{\n2548 |                 \"fields\": [\n2549 |                     {{\n2550 |                         \"name\": \"index\",\n2551 |                         \"type\": \"string\"\n2552 |                     }},\n2553 |                     {{\n2554 |                         \"name\": \"col 1\",\n2555 |                         \"type\": \"string\"\n2556 |                     }},\n2557 |                     {{\n2558 |                         \"name\": \"col 2\",\n2559 |                         \"type\": \"string\"\n2560 |                     }}\n2561 |                 ],\n2562 |                 \"primaryKey\": [\n2563 |                     \"index\"\n2564 |                 ],\n2565 |                 \"pandas_version\": \"1.4.0\"\n2566 |             }},\n2567 |             \"data\": [\n2568 |                 {{\n2569 |                     \"index\": \"row 1\",\n2570 |                     \"col 1\": \"a\",\n2571 |                     \"col 2\": \"b\"\n2572 |                 }},\n2573 |                 {{\n2574 |                     \"index\": \"row 2\",\n2575 |                     \"col 1\": \"c\",\n2576 |                     \"col 2\": \"d\"\n2577 |                 }}\n2578 |             ]\n2579 |         }}\n2580 |         \"\"\"\n2581 |         from pandas.io import json\n2582 | \n2583 |         if date_format is None and orient == \"table\":\n2584 |             date_format = \"iso\"\n2585 |         elif date_format is None:\n2586 |             date_format = \"epoch\"\n2587 |             dtypes = self.dtypes if self.ndim == 2 else [self.dtype]\n2588 |             if any(lib.is_np_dtype(dtype, \"mM\") for dtype in dtypes):\n2589 |                 warnings.warn(\n2590 |                     \"The default 'epoch' date format is deprecated and will be removed \"\n2591 |                     \"in a future version, please use 'iso' date format instead.\",\n2592 |                     FutureWarning,\n2593 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n2594 |                 )\n2595 |         elif date_format == \"epoch\":\n2596 |             # GH#57063\n2597 |             warnings.warn(\n2598 |                 \"'epoch' date format is deprecated and will be removed in a future \"\n2599 |                 \"version, please use 'iso' date format instead.\",\n2600 |                 FutureWarning,\n2601 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n2602 |             )\n2603 | \n2604 |         config.is_nonnegative_int(indent)\n2605 |         indent = indent or 0\n2606 | \n2607 |         return json.to_json(\n2608 |             path_or_buf=path_or_buf,\n2609 |             obj=self,\n2610 |             orient=orient,\n2611 |             date_format=date_format,\n2612 |             double_precision=double_precision,\n2613 |             force_ascii=force_ascii,\n2614 |             date_unit=date_unit,\n2615 |             default_handler=default_handler,\n2616 |             lines=lines,\n2617 |             compression=compression,\n2618 |             index=index,\n2619 |             indent=indent,\n2620 |             storage_options=storage_options,\n2621 |             mode=mode,\n2622 |         )\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.to_excel`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2150 | def to_excel(\n2151 |         self,\n2152 |         excel_writer: FilePath | WriteExcelBuffer | ExcelWriter,\n2153 |         *,\n2154 |         sheet_name: str = \"Sheet1\",\n2155 |         na_rep: str = \"\",\n2156 |         float_format: str | None = None,\n2157 |         columns: Sequence[Hashable] | None = None,\n2158 |         header: Sequence[Hashable] | bool = True,\n2159 |         index: bool = True,\n2160 |         index_label: IndexLabel | None = None,\n2161 |         startrow: int = 0,\n2162 |         startcol: int = 0,\n2163 |         engine: Literal[\"openpyxl\", \"xlsxwriter\"] | None = None,\n2164 |         merge_cells: bool = True,\n2165 |         inf_rep: str = \"inf\",\n2166 |         freeze_panes: tuple[int, int] | None = None,\n2167 |         storage_options: StorageOptions | None = None,\n2168 |         engine_kwargs: dict[str, Any] | None = None,\n2169 |     ) -> None:\n2170 |         \"\"\"\n2171 |         Write {klass} to an Excel sheet.\n2172 | \n2173 |         To write a single {klass} to an Excel .xlsx file it is only necessary to\n2174 |         specify a target file name. To write to multiple sheets it is necessary to\n2175 |         create an `ExcelWriter` object with a target file name, and specify a sheet\n2176 |         in the file to write to.\n2177 | \n2178 |         Multiple sheets may be written to by specifying unique `sheet_name`.\n2179 |         With all data written to the file it is necessary to save the changes.\n2180 |         Note that creating an `ExcelWriter` object with a file name that already\n2181 |         exists will result in the contents of the existing file being erased.\n2182 | \n2183 |         Parameters\n2184 |         ----------\n2185 |         excel_writer : path-like, file-like, or ExcelWriter object\n2186 |             File path or existing ExcelWriter.\n2187 |         sheet_name : str, default 'Sheet1'\n2188 |             Name of sheet which will contain DataFrame.\n2189 |         na_rep : str, default ''\n2190 |             Missing data representation.\n2191 |         float_format : str, optional\n2192 |             Format string for floating point numbers. For example\n2193 |             ``float_format=\"%.2f\"`` will format 0.1234 to 0.12.\n2194 |         columns : sequence or list of str, optional\n2195 |             Columns to write.\n2196 |         header : bool or list of str, default True\n2197 |             Write out the column names. If a list of string is given it is\n2198 |             assumed to be aliases for the column names.\n2199 |         index : bool, default True\n2200 |             Write row names (index).\n2201 |         index_label : str or sequence, optional\n2202 |             Column label for index column(s) if desired. If not specified, and\n2203 |             `header` and `index` are True, then the index names are used. A\n2204 |             sequence should be given if the DataFrame uses MultiIndex.\n2205 |         startrow : int, default 0\n2206 |             Upper left cell row to dump data frame.\n2207 |         startcol : int, default 0\n2208 |             Upper left cell column to dump data frame.\n2209 |         engine : str, optional\n2210 |             Write engine to use, 'openpyxl' or 'xlsxwriter'. You can also set this\n2211 |             via the options ``io.excel.xlsx.writer`` or\n2212 |             ``io.excel.xlsm.writer``.\n2213 | \n2214 |         merge_cells : bool or 'columns', default False\n2215 |             If True, write MultiIndex index and columns as merged cells.\n2216 |             If 'columns', merge MultiIndex column cells only.\n2217 |         {encoding_parameter}\n2218 |         inf_rep : str, default 'inf'\n2219 |             Representation for infinity (there is no native representation for\n2220 |             infinity in Excel).\n2221 |         {verbose_parameter}\n2222 |         freeze_panes : tuple of int (length 2), optional\n2223 |             Specifies the one-based bottommost row and rightmost column that\n2224 |             is to be frozen.\n2225 |         {storage_options}\n2226 | \n2227 |             .. versionadded:: {storage_options_versionadded}\n2228 |         {extra_parameters}\n2229 |         See Also\n2230 |         --------\n2231 |         to_csv : Write DataFrame to a comma-separated values (csv) file.\n2232 |         ExcelWriter : Class for writing DataFrame objects into excel sheets.\n2233 |         read_excel : Read an Excel file into a pandas DataFrame.\n2234 |         read_csv : Read a comma-separated values (csv) file into DataFrame.\n2235 |         io.formats.style.Styler.to_excel : Add styles to Excel sheet.\n2236 | \n2237 |         Notes\n2238 |         -----\n2239 |         For compatibility with :meth:`~DataFrame.to_csv`,\n2240 |         to_excel serializes lists and dicts to strings before writing.\n2241 | \n2242 |         Once a workbook has been saved it is not possible to write further\n2243 |         data without rewriting the whole workbook.\n2244 | \n2245 |         pandas will check the number of rows, columns,\n2246 |         and cell character count does not exceed Excel's limitations.\n2247 |         All other limitations must be checked by the user.\n2248 | \n2249 |         Examples\n2250 |         --------\n2251 | \n2252 |         Create, write to and save a workbook:\n2253 | \n2254 |         >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame(\n2255 |         ...     [[\"a\", \"b\"], [\"c\", \"d\"]],\n2256 |         ...     index=[\"row 1\", \"row 2\"],\n2257 |         ...     columns=[\"col 1\", \"col 2\"],\n2258 |         ... )\n2259 |         >>> df1.to_excel(\"output.xlsx\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n2260 | \n2261 |         To specify the sheet name:\n2262 | \n2263 |         >>> df1.to_excel(\"output.xlsx\", sheet_name=\"Sheet_name_1\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n2264 | \n2265 |         If you wish to write to more than one sheet in the workbook, it is\n2266 |         necessary to specify an ExcelWriter object:\n2267 | \n2268 |         >>> df2 = df1.copy()\n2269 |         >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"output.xlsx\") as writer:  # doctest: +SKIP\n2270 |         ...     df1.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet_name_1\")\n2271 |         ...     df2.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet_name_2\")\n2272 | \n2273 |         ExcelWriter can also be used to append to an existing Excel file:\n2274 | \n2275 |         >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"output.xlsx\", mode=\"a\") as writer:  # doctest: +SKIP\n2276 |         ...     df1.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet_name_3\")\n2277 | \n2278 | 